i.github.com/users/Vatsa10/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vatsa10/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vatsa10/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vatsa10/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Vatsa10","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vatsa10/Harbour/commits/4d679b28cceb8e8856bdb7c0df28f00d75322ab4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T05:49:58Z","email":"vatsajoshi2@gmail.com","name":"Vatsa Joshi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T05:49:58Z","email":"vatsajoshi2@gmail.com","name":"Vatsa Joshi"},"message":"fix(tool-provider): enforce the catalog''s grant rule at dispatch, not just at discovery\n\nPhase-4 review I5. Discovery derives what a role may write from its\nexplicit object grants; record-crud enforcement reads the composed\nrolesPermissions cache, which grants every role full CRUD on any isSystem\nobject. The invariant ''a zero-permission role cannot write'' was therefore\nheld only by discovery never emitting the tool, with no check on the\nenforcement side -- exactly the regression Task 13 caught.\n\nDatabaseToolProvider.isCrudOperationPermitted exposes the catalog''s rule,\nand ToolExecutorService.dispatchDatabaseCrud consults it before touching\na record-crud service, returning PERMISSION_DENIED instead.\n\nVerified by mutation: making the check return true unconditionally fails\nthree of the six new tests.","tree":{"sha":"e162c92cbbf3ce2472bc49bd561822435ef9c2c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vatsa10/Harbour/git/trees/e162c92cbbf3ce2472bc49bd561822435ef9c2c0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Vatsa10/Harbour/git/commits/4d679b28cceb8e8856bdb7c0df28f00d75322ab4","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/123623531?v=4","events_ur