s/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/855f9f1c6fa6fbfa4f11ad3f996d0f67c36b21e0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-02T08:05:16Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-02T08:05:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Name the check Evals / Rules check (#28)\n\nThe workflow is Evals and its job is the Rules check, so the pull request''s\nchecks list reads \"Evals / Rules check\" instead of \"Rules check / grade the\ndiff\". The comment the job writes when the check could not run is titled\nEvals too, like the one the check renders.\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"187e3a605106c4accee504db63f88ab49b820762","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/187e3a605106c4accee504db63f88ab49b820762"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/855f9f1c6fa6fbfa4f11ad3f996d0f67c36b21e0","verification":{"payload":"tree 187e3a605106c4accee504db63f88ab49b820762\nparent 071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790928316 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790928316 +0200\n\nName the check Evals / Rules check (#28)\n\nThe workflow is Evals and its job is the Rules check, so the pull request''s\nchecks list reads \"Evals / Rules check\" instead of \"Rules check / grade the\ndiff\". The comment the job writes when the check could not run is titled\nEvals too, like the one the check renders.\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqv2W8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4fwQAKZR6XafMlRiJX7htfHzMePo\n1ARXk7xlXLIeMP3GC5gOwuT/Mmj/wccIGMCMFif5Iqtj3CjMi3TJPQ58nM5CcMHx\nFFTqF/cQL2lU+3OhtGTt7rOy3rGn41xU3iZlasmSgeEmx33F3B0Riqvd/K+fxGTF\nRf2ODT4PY3ghShxWPIoo5sdMEqPqTPaFbSpv/gaFDI5R2NHcBeP8oDK30RUX/K+B\n3Kfzz4pPhBr2LVnNHYujTWt+lVv/bHCFSzDxzJG/chGcsgzq9YjtX4SVrepyhW/x\nOOLFGc1r0dLKCGvIY/ywErYnvNNrDRXCDhSsFIzhztFlSvVf7JU8XgyHAEYQbhZ8\nfKfRlvatxH+OX3KZtNdMTs7MNKM0SsrexZmi1beKjTkkpRvlXMwoxeh5rSWo2kqe\n7LblAuOLSCH62zqQzEP+FJuCqdEwdlVzKhkgm4laOYIU99TghqI2a1hczr9M0fSJ\n9hWS33t10TwKtg/3oKJuMYuJGojWb2GJJbHq8tF0UnR2A0mYAgA0Nwu4033U9cTl\njYlyL8mKqn2/brI89yz878P/g/BlPSf/qIsVl3GtORlgZyudJwCr2ZnT0TLXrOAT\nU8lSbEa9xY7y5fD8k7yb0W1TZkzusciUj/O4FPwyxcQG+OmX5DNKZwzEJF9KmOGX\nxHNVxQAH/jMeEXuTTS3r\n=Bvu2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-02T08:05:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/855f9f1c6fa6fbfa4f11ad3f996d0f67c36b21e0","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKDg1NWY5ZjFjNmZhNmZiZmE0ZjExYWQzZjk5NmQwZjY3YzM2YjIxZTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3","sha":"071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3"}],"sha":"855f9f1c6fa6fbfa4f11ad3f996d0f67c36b21e0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/855f9f1c6fa6fbfa4f11ad3f996d0f67c36b21e0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T15:30:12Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T15:30:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Send the hand-written Sentry signals to Datadog too (#25)\n\n* Add Datadog RUM, browser logs and source-map uploads beside Sentry\n\nThe shell starts Datadog RUM and browser logs next to Sentry, so the two\ncan be compared on the same sessions. Both remotes report through that\none init, with the Datadog SDKs shared as federation singletons. Views\nare named by route through Datadog''s React Router wrapper, and errors\nthat Sentry''s boundaries and the query cache catch are reported to\nDatadog too.\n\n`yarn build` uploads each build''s source maps to Datadog when\nDATADOG_API_KEY is set, under the same version as Sentry''s release, and\ndeletes them from dist/ once both uploads have read them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Continue the RUM trace through both backend services in Datadog\n\nBrowser RUM now writes a W3C traceparent on every call to /api/, and\nboth services continue it into Datadog APM beside Sentry, so a save is\none Datadog trace from the RUM resource through the account API, its\nPostgres queries and the notifications service.\n\nThe two services reach a local Datadog Agent (yarn datadog:start) by\ndifferent roads. The account API uses OpenTelemetry over OTLP, because\nDatadog''s .NET tracer has no macOS build. The notifications service\nuses dd-trace, because Sentry''s Node SDK already owns the global\nOpenTelemetry provider. Neither reads nor writes `baggage`, which stays\nSentry''s, and neither traces Sentry''s own uploads. docs/adr/0008\nrecords the decision.\n\nBoth are off unless DD_AGENT_HOST is set, so CI traces nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Datadog RUM to the mobile app beside Sentry\n\nThe Expo app starts Datadog''s React Native SDK through DatadogProvider\nwhen EXPO_PUBLIC_DATADOG_APPLICATION_ID and _CLIENT_TOKEN are set, and\nnever under jest. Views are named by Expo Router route, failed queries\nand mutations are reported next to Sentry''s, requests made through\nExpo''s native fetch are tracked, and calls to the account API carry a\nW3C traceparent so they join the backend trace.\n\nA native Release build uploads its dSYMs, source maps and Proguard\nmappings to Datadog when DATADOG_API_KEY is set at prebuild: app.config\nadds the expo-datadog plugin only then, so a build without a key still\nsucceeds. The Metro config stamps bundles with the debug IDs those maps\nare matched by.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Send the hand-written Sentry signals to Datadog too\n\nThe account save, its log, and the backend''s errors and custom span reached\nSentry only. Each now has a Datadog twin:\n\n- Web: the `account.save` span is mirrored by a RUM action of the same name,\n  which ties the PUT and its backend trace to the save. \"Account updated\" is\n  also sent through `datadogLogs`, and that log is what the two Sentry metrics\n  become in Datadog, as log-based metrics defined there.\n- `@datadog/browser-logs` joins the federation singletons, so the account\n  remote logs through the shell''s initialised copy.\n- Notifications: `notification.send` gets a dd-trace span, and an unhandled\n  error is tagged on the server span so Error Tracking can group it.\n- Account API: the ASP.NET Core instrumentation records exceptions, for the\n  same reason.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Profile the notifications service with Datadog instead of Sentry\n\nTwo profilers sampling one V8 isolate would skew each other, so it is one at\na time. When dd-trace starts it now turns on Datadog''s continuous profiler, and\ninstrument.ts leaves Sentry''s off whenever DD_PROFILING_ENABLED is \"true\".\nWithout an Agent, Sentry profiles as before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Profile the web app with Datadog RUM instead of Sentry\n\nThe same rule as the notifications service: one profiler per page. When\nDatadog RUM runs it profiles every session, and the shell''s Sentry init\nleaves `browserProfilingIntegration` out. Without Datadog, Sentry profiles\nas before. The `Document-Policy: js-profiling` header both need is already\nsent by the shell''s Vite config.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Datadog logs, per-remote attribution and version alignment\n\nBackend logs now reach Datadog beside the traces, joined to them. The\naccount API sends its ILogger output over OTLP, filtered to Information\nand up (Warning for the framework and HttpClient). The notifications\nservice sends JSON lines stamped with dd.trace_id to a UDP port the Agent\ncollects from (datadog-logs.yaml), because dd-trace''s OpenTelemetry logs\nwould take Sentry''s global context manager. docs/adr/0008 records it.\n\nErrors in the account API carry error.* tags, which Error Tracking groups\nby, and the git commit and repository URL from the assembly, so Datadog\ncan show each frame''s code.\n\nEvery part now reports the same version. `accounts:start` and\n`notifications:start` pass GIT_COMMIT, which each service uses as\nSENTRY_RELEASE when .env sets none, and the mobile app reports the commit\nand files its native uploads under it.\n\nOn the web, each build stamps its chunks with its own Datadog service, so\na remote''s errors, long tasks and vitals are filed under that remote.\nFetches are attributed in `beforeSend` (datadog-owner.ts), since Sentry''s\nfetch wrapper hides the caller from the SDK. Source maps are matched by\ndebug ID instead of service, version and URL. A federation runtime plugin\nmeasures each remote''s load time, reported as a `remote.load` vital, and\na save the form schema refuses is recorded as `account.save_rejected`.\n\n`yarn server:start` starts the database and both services, and `yarn\nstart` and `yarn preview` both use it; `yarn preview:web` is the builds\nalone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Track the home page''s account preview as a Datadog RUM operation\n\n\"The visitor saw whose account this is\" is now a RUM operation,\n`account.preview_displayed`: started when the home page mounts, a success\nwhen the preview is on screen with its data, a failure when the boundary\ncatches the remote or the account failing, and abandoned when the visitor\nleaves first. Its success rate is what an SLO can be set on.\n\nFor that moment to be one the page can see, the preview suspends on the\naccount (`useSuspenseAccount`) and throws when it cannot be loaded, instead\nof rendering its own loading and error states. It now waits on the same\nSuspense and boundary as the federated module, and the wording of both\nstates is the home page''s.\n\nOperations are experimental in the browser SDK, so the shell enables\n`feature_operation_vital`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run Datadog Synthetic tests against the local preview builds\n\nTwo Synthetic tests - the account API through the shell''s proxy, and the\naccount page in a browser - run against `yarn preview`, from a private\nlocation on this machine on a schedule, and on demand with\n`yarn synthetics:run` through datadog-ci''s tunnel. The tests live in\nDatadog; synthetics/README.md is the record of what they contain and how to\nrecreate them.\n\nThe private location is a worker in compose.yaml behind a socat sidecar that\nforwards 4173-4175 to the host, so the same localhost URLs work from the\ncontainer and through the tunnel. The previews now listen on 127.0.0.1\n(`previewHost`): left to \"localhost\", Node binds ::1 alone on macOS, which a\ncontainer cannot reach. The location''s config holds secret keys and is\ngitignored.\n\ndatadog-ci reads DD_API_KEY, DD_APP_KEY and DATADOG_SITE; synthetics/\ndatadog-env.ts maps them from the names .env already uses, plus a new\nDATADOG_APP_KEY.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the mobile build out of `yarn preview`\n\n`turbo run preview` depends on `build` in every workspace, including the\nones with no preview script: `@demo/mobile#preview` does not exist, but its\n`build` - `expo export --platform ios` - ran anyway on every preview. The\nscript now filters to the three web apps, the same list `//#e2e` names.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Time the account''s React components as Datadog RUM vitals\n\nAccountPage, EditAccountPage and AccountPreview are wrapped in\n`UNSTABLE_ReactComponentTracker` where the remote exposes them (pages.ts,\npreview.ts), the way pages.ts already applies Sentry''s `withProfiler`. Each\ncommit of those components becomes a `reactComponentRender` vital on the\nRUM view, named after the component and split into render, layout-effect\nand effect phases - a React component on the view''s waterfall, next to the\nrequest it waited for.\n\nIt times rendering only: a component that suspends is timed on the commit\nthat shows it, and the wait for its data stays the fetch on the waterfall.\nThe shell''s init sends the vitals through the singleton\n@datadog/browser-rum-react, now a dependency of the account app.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start Datadog RUM views when a navigation begins, not when its URL commits\n\nThe `createBrowserRouter` from `@datadog/browser-rum-react` starts a view\nwhen the pathname changes, but a data router resolves a route''s `lazy`\nbefore committing the URL. A remote''s chunks were therefore filed under\nthe page being left, and the new view''s loading time started late.\n\n`trackRumViews` subscribes to React Router''s own router and starts each\nview from its loading phase, named by the matched route template.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bring the mobile app level with the web on Datadog and Sentry signals\n\nSession replay on both SDKs and both platforms, a deliberate crash on saving\nthe account behind VITE_CRASH_ACCOUNT_SAVE / EXPO_PUBLIC_CRASH_ACCOUNT_SAVE,\nand on mobile: logs and caught errors written to both tools through one\nfunction each, an error boundary on the account tab, view loading times,\nthe save as a RUM action, accessibility labels that name each tap, and\nSentry''s source-map upload beside Datadog''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"412c66dcca9119ebcc0d969e62209b8be4e0fc9f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/412c66dcca9119ebcc0d969e62209b8be4e0fc9f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/071f0e9d8b3b668bf8d3b4f5d58b3f2a79396af3","verification":{"payload":"tree 412c66dcca9119ebcc0d969e62209b8be4e0fc9f\nparent 48a8c18eb4c78f62cd007dd56a105c59a55491ef\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790868612 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790868612 +0200\n\nSend the hand-written Sentry signals to Datadog too (#25)\n\n* Add Datadog RUM, browser logs and source-map uploads beside Sentry\n\nThe shell starts Datadog RUM and browser logs next to Sentry, so the two\ncan be compared on the same sessions. Both remotes report through that\none init, with the Datadog SDKs shared as federation singletons. Views\nare named by route through Datadog''s React Router wrapper, and errors\nthat Sentry''s boundaries and the query cache catch are reported to\nDatadog too.\n\n`yarn build` uploads each build''s source maps to Datadog when\nDATADOG_API_KEY is set, under the same version as Sentry''s release, and\ndeletes them from dist/ once both uploads have read them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Continue the RUM trace through both backend services in Datadog\n\nBrowser RUM now writes a W3C traceparent on every call to /api/, and\nboth services continue it into Datadog APM beside Sentry, so a save is\none Datadog trace from the RUM resource through the account API, its\nPostgres queries and the notifications service.\n\nThe two services reach a local Datadog Agent (yarn datadog:start) by\ndifferent roads. The account API uses OpenTelemetry over OTLP, because\nDatadog''s .NET tracer has no macOS build. The notifications service\nuses dd-trace, because Sentry''s Node SDK already owns the global\nOpenTelemetry provider. Neither reads nor writes `baggage`, which stays\nSentry''s, and neither traces Sentry''s own uploads. docs/adr/0008\nrecords the decision.\n\nBoth are off unless DD_AGENT_HOST is set, so CI traces nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Datadog RUM to the mobile app beside Sentry\n\nThe Expo app starts Datadog''s React Native SDK through DatadogProvider\nwhen EXPO_PUBLIC_DATADOG_APPLICATION_ID and _CLIENT_TOKEN are set, and\nnever under jest. Views are named by Expo Router route, failed queries\nand mutations are reported next to Sentry''s, requests made through\nExpo''s native fetch are tracked, and calls to the account API carry a\nW3C traceparent so they join the backend trace.\n\nA native Release build uploads its dSYMs, source maps and Proguard\nmappings to Datadog when DATADOG_API_KEY is set at prebuild: app.config\nadds the expo-datadog plugin only then, so a build without a key still\nsucceeds. The Metro config stamps bundles with the debug IDs those maps\nare matched by.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Send the hand-written Sentry signals to Datadog too\n\nThe account save, its log, and the backend''s errors and custom span reached\nSentry only. Each now has a Datadog twin:\n\n- Web: the `account.save` span is mirrored by a RUM action of the same name,\n  which ties the PUT and its backend trace to the save. \"Account updated\" is\n  also sent through `datadogLogs`, and that log is what the two Sentry metrics\n  become in Datadog, as log-based metrics defined there.\n- `@datadog/browser-logs` joins the federation singletons, so the account\n  remote logs through the shell''s initialised copy.\n- Notifications: `notification.send` gets a dd-trace span, and an unhandled\n  error is tagged on the server span so Error Tracking can group it.\n- Account API: the ASP.NET Core instrumentation records exceptions, for the\n  same reason.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Profile the notifications service with Datadog instead of Sentry\n\nTwo profilers sampling one V8 isolate would skew each other, so it is one at\na time. When dd-trace starts it now turns on Datadog''s continuous profiler, and\ninstrument.ts leaves Sentry''s off whenever DD_PROFILING_ENABLED is \"true\".\nWithout an Agent, Sentry profiles as before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Profile the web app with Datadog RUM instead of Sentry\n\nThe same rule as the notifications service: one profiler per page. When\nDatadog RUM runs it profiles every session, and the shell''s Sentry init\nleaves `browserProfilingIntegration` out. Without Datadog, Sentry profiles\nas before. The `Document-Policy: js-profiling` header both need is already\nsent by the shell''s Vite config.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Datadog logs, per-remote attribution and version alignment\n\nBackend logs now reach Datadog beside the traces, joined to them. The\naccount API sends its ILogger output over OTLP, filtered to Information\nand up (Warning for the framework and HttpClient). The notifications\nservice sends JSON lines stamped with dd.trace_id to a UDP port the Agent\ncollects from (datadog-logs.yaml), because dd-trace''s OpenTelemetry logs\nwould take Sentry''s global context manager. docs/adr/0008 records it.\n\nErrors in the account API carry error.* tags, which Error Tracking groups\nby, and the git commit and repository URL from the assembly, so Datadog\ncan show each frame''s code.\n\nEvery part now reports the same version. `accounts:start` and\n`notifications:start` pass GIT_COMMIT, which each service uses as\nSENTRY_RELEASE when .env sets none, and the mobile app reports the commit\nand files its native uploads under it.\n\nOn the web, each build stamps its chunks with its own Datadog service, so\na remote''s errors, long tasks and vitals are filed under that remote.\nFetches are attributed in `beforeSend` (datadog-owner.ts), since Sentry''s\nfetch wrapper hides the caller from the SDK. Source maps are matched by\ndebug ID instead of service, version and URL. A federation runtime plugin\nmeasures each remote''s load time, reported as a `remote.load` vital, and\na save the form schema refuses is recorded as `account.save_rejected`.\n\n`yarn server:start` starts the database and both services, and `yarn\nstart` and `yarn preview` both use it; `yarn preview:web` is the builds\nalone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Track the home page''s account preview as a Datadog RUM operation\n\n\"The visitor saw whose account this is\" is now a RUM operation,\n`account.preview_displayed`: started when the home page mounts, a success\nwhen the preview is on screen with its data, a failure when the boundary\ncatches the remote or the account failing, and abandoned when the visitor\nleaves first. Its success rate is what an SLO can be set on.\n\nFor that moment to be one the page can see, the preview suspends on the\naccount (`useSuspenseAccount`) and throws when it cannot be loaded, instead\nof rendering its own loading and error states. It now waits on the same\nSuspense and boundary as the federated module, and the wording of both\nstates is the home page''s.\n\nOperations are experimental in the browser SDK, so the shell enables\n`feature_operation_vital`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run Datadog Synthetic tests against the local preview builds\n\nTwo Synthetic tests - the account API through the shell''s proxy, and the\naccount page in a browser - run against `yarn preview`, from a private\nlocation on this machine on a schedule, and on demand with\n`yarn synthetics:run` through datadog-ci''s tunnel. The tests live in\nDatadog; synthetics/README.md is the record of what they contain and how to\nrecreate them.\n\nThe private location is a worker in compose.yaml behind a socat sidecar that\nforwards 4173-4175 to the host, so the same localhost URLs work from the\ncontainer and through the tunnel. The previews now listen on 127.0.0.1\n(`previewHost`): left to \"localhost\", Node binds ::1 alone on macOS, which a\ncontainer cannot reach. The location''s config holds secret keys and is\ngitignored.\n\ndatadog-ci reads DD_API_KEY, DD_APP_KEY and DATADOG_SITE; synthetics/\ndatadog-env.ts maps them from the names .env already uses, plus a new\nDATADOG_APP_KEY.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the mobile build out of `yarn preview`\n\n`turbo run preview` depends on `build` in every workspace, including the\nones with no preview script: `@demo/mobile#preview` does not exist, but its\n`build` - `expo export --platform ios` - ran anyway on every preview. The\nscript now filters to the three web apps, the same list `//#e2e` names.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Time the account''s React components as Datadog RUM vitals\n\nAccountPage, EditAccountPage and AccountPreview are wrapped in\n`UNSTABLE_ReactComponentTracker` where the remote exposes them (pages.ts,\npreview.ts), the way pages.ts already applies Sentry''s `withProfiler`. Each\ncommit of those components becomes a `reactComponentRender` vital on the\nRUM view, named after the component and split into render, layout-effect\nand effect phases - a React component on the view''s waterfall, next to the\nrequest it waited for.\n\nIt times rendering only: a component that suspends is timed on the commit\nthat shows it, and the wait for its data stays the fetch on the waterfall.\nThe shell''s init sends the vitals through the singleton\n@datadog/browser-rum-react, now a dependency of the account app.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start Datadog RUM views when a navigation begins, not when its URL commits\n\nThe `createBrowserRouter` from `@datadog/browser-rum-react` starts a view\nwhen the pathname changes, but a data router resolves a route''s `lazy`\nbefore committing the URL. A remote''s chunks were therefore filed under\nthe page being left, and the new view''s loading time started late.\n\n`trackRumViews` subscribes to React Router''s own router and starts each\nview from its loading phase, named by the matched route template.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bring the mobile app level with the web on Datadog and Sentry signals\n\nSession replay on both SDKs and both platforms, a deliberate crash on saving\nthe account behind VITE_CRASH_ACCOUNT_SAVE / EXPO_PUBLIC_CRASH_ACCOUNT_SAVE,\nand on mobile: logs and caught errors written to both tools through one\nfunction each, an error boundary on the account tab, view loading times,\nthe save as a RUM action, accessibility labels that name each tap, and\nSentry''s source-map upload beside Datadog''s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvnyECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAq9EQAHdQ05pExkzOKDxQBeCwib2