,"parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9","sha":"b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9"}],"sha":"af2b4085cc08e02d048af1c5bf10dfa78852949a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/af2b4085cc08e02d048af1c5bf10dfa78852949a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T10:17:26Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T10:17:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Run Lighthouse CI over the shell and its remotes on every PR (#18)\n\n* Run Lighthouse CI over the shell and its remotes on every PR\n\n`yarn lighthouse` audits /, /account and /account/edit on the preview shell,\nthree runs each, keeping the median. It audits the shell on 4173 rather than a\ndist/, because a remote''s build holds no page anyone visits, and it has no\n`startServerCommand`, because a page needs five processes up and one ready\npattern cannot say when all five are. lighthouserc.yml says how to start them\nlocally; .github/workflows/lighthouse.yml starts them in CI.\n\nAccessibility errors below 0.9, since it is read off the DOM and scores the\nsame every run. Performance and best practices only warn: they are timed on a\nshared runner, and a slow neighbour should not fail a PR that changed nothing.\nReports go to temporary public storage, which holds only the demo account''s\ngenerated data.\n\nIt is a workflow of its own rather than a job in ci.yml, which stays the gate\nAGENTS.md defines.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep Lighthouse history in a local LHCI server, and audit only the pages a PR reaches\n\nThe results now land in a Lighthouse CI server that `yarn lhci:start` runs in\nDocker (compose.yaml), which keeps every run and compares a branch with main.\nNothing hosts that server, so CI cannot reach it: CI keeps uploading to\ntemporary public storage for the PR''s links, and saves the raw results and\ntheir build context as an artifact. `yarn lhci:start` pulls those artifacts\ninto the local server once it is up (lighthouse/pull.ts), skipping the runs\nit already has. The status checks now go through the run''s own GITHUB_TOKEN,\nso no GitHub App is needed.\n\nA PR audits only the pages its changes reach. lighthouse/run.ts lists each\npage with the packages it is built from, and asks `turbo ls --affected` which\npackages changed; Turborepo adds their dependents, and marks everything when a\nglobal dependency changes. `/` also lists the account, because home embeds the\naccount''s preview over federation, which the package graph cannot see. The\nworkflow picks the pages in a first job and skips the audit job when there\nare none. main always audits every page, so the dashboard keeps a complete\nbaseline, and so does a PR that changes the audit itself.\n\nThe config moved to lighthouse/, next to the two scripts, and no longer lists\nthe URLs: run.ts is their one source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ab0c4807dfbc4ddca611d8c0b104870b16416cde","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/ab0c4807dfbc4ddca611d8c0b104870b16416cde"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9","verification":{"payload":"tree ab0c4807dfbc4ddca611d8c0b104870b16416cde\nparent da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790331446 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790331446 +0200\n\nRun Lighthouse CI over the shell and its remotes on every PR (#18)\n\n* Run Lighthouse CI over the shell and its remotes on every PR\n\n`yarn lighthouse` audits /, /account and /account/edit on the preview shell,\nthree runs each, keeping the median. It audits the shell on 4173 rather than a\ndist/, because a remote''s build holds no page anyone visits, and it has no\n`startServerCommand`, because a page needs five processes up and one ready\npattern cannot say when all five are. lighthouserc.yml says how to start them\nlocally; .github/workflows/lighthouse.yml starts them in CI.\n\nAccessibility errors below 0.9, since it is read off the DOM and scores the\nsame every run. Performance and best practices only warn: they are timed on a\nshared runner, and a slow neighbour should not fail a PR that changed nothing.\nReports go to temporary public storage, which holds only the demo account''s\ngenerated data.\n\nIt is a workflow of its own rather than a job in ci.yml, which stays the gate\nAGENTS.md defines.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep Lighthouse history in a local LHCI server, and audit only the pages a PR reaches\n\nThe results now land in a Lighthouse CI server that `yarn lhci:start` runs in\nDocker (compose.yaml), which keeps every run and compares a branch with main.\nNothing hosts that server, so CI cannot reach it: CI keeps uploading to\ntemporary public storage for the PR''s links, and saves the raw results and\ntheir build context as an artifact. `yarn lhci:start` pulls those artifacts\ninto the local server once it is up (lighthouse/pull.ts), skipping the runs\nit already has. The status checks now go through the run''s own GITHUB_TOKEN,\nso no GitHub App is needed.\n\nA PR audits only the pages its changes reach. lighthouse/run.ts lists each\npage with the packages it is built from, and asks `turbo ls --affected` which\npackages changed; Turborepo adds their dependents, and marks everything when a\nglobal dependency changes. `/` also lists the account, because home embeds the\naccount''s preview over federation, which the package graph cannot see. The\nworkflow picks the pages in a first job and skips the audit job when there\nare none. main always audits every page, so the dashboard keeps a complete\nbaseline, and so does a PR that changes the audit itself.\n\nThe config moved to lighthouse/, next to the two scripts, and no longer lists\nthe URLs: run.ts is their one source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtko2CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGTQQADLX39eBeAkE0+14biJf5u5z\nMCiaqLtBCye8Jx/W2tEdwsOCK/RQClhBMqQFsrR65svbN/MXuf82cdxwGQ2VJpZd\nmiij5zArEqoAepe8sGvLopwdbqFgOd5OCnBQLPVZnj3l0XCM+cfmGppgCdjUbLas\n5ElhCauWmyEenPE9hQlTp8uPey4wtmg7nc3TVImwzkU/BlF3Iu/cxgrPkOH7YGjJ\nXZsHT20B/tEe72ex+fCuQkEPJXDpmd6cCkPM0m51dUX3qNL6vGARyyE8S/VY0FK7\nY1+YLGq+ZtezLPKcF18u/Npj8Nn6755fDbWiXVEQ43zm7tthEe0nWW8I03/7fLW7\nreNfbSTOV5IebE6IjLiWo0+1r0ByzdfRo/XG3Sek5rmo6g4ZcfCgYyWJ5zfW92Xy\nDOVlWc0Mx68yS/l+4YKj6fEvL2/itPd0K3WYRWL3bko3UNnb7KfkACq+2GRP4zir\naU/HC66ypzHVFcwL/Xyf8Ra0EYjbGd/7nwbDaVXj0mPLMG5jgII6sIAjMVPaW2xZ\nwOIjeYzDik07aTGL1JZkcl78msetcyp4SDJAz2v5ixQO9Ck7eaPZIXi3ieEACete\nVJ2B4aqrqVaK8p7LNf8X3RAoUHoyHLgeHF+cMOTjA/UT47DrA3wq4EPRFiegWf3X\nKFvBGuB/VrVKtEkXBHR5\n=6G+f\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T10:17:26Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKGI5YjVjMjZlMWEwZDYyODdiZDdmYmRjZDI3NDMwYjRkYTY3ODJmZDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc","sha":"da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc"}],"sha":"b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/b9b5c26e1a0d6287bd7fbdcd27430b4da6782fd9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T09:15:07Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T09:15:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Move the mobile app to @sentry/react-native 8, and trace its fetch by hand (#17)\n\n* Give the first native test enough time for a cold transform cache\n\nThe 30s limit from the previous commit passed on two CI runs and failed on\nthe third. The first test of each file is where babel transforms expo-router,\nReact Native and the screens, and CI always starts with an empty transform\ncache. Measured cold, that test takes 25s on a laptop and 30 to 45s on a CI\nrunner; warm, it takes 2.6s.\n\nThe limit is now 120s, so it covers a cold cache with room to spare and\nstill catches a test that truly hangs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Move the mobile app to @sentry/react-native 8, and trace its fetch by hand\n\nExpo 57 pins `~7.11.0`, and 7.x stamps spans from `performance.timeOrigin`,\nwhich on React Native 0.86 under iOS falls behind the wall clock by every hour\nthe machine has slept. A simulator left open for days sent navigation spans\ndated days in the past: Sentry accepted them with a 200, and no search for\n\"the last hour\" ever found them. 8.25 stopped trusting that clock\n(getsentry/sentry-react-native#6654). `expo.install.exclude` keeps\n`expo install --fix` from putting 7.x back.\n\n`traceFetch: true` is set by hand because the SDK''s own guess is wrong here.\nExpo 57''s global fetch is `expo/fetch`, which bypasses the XMLHttpRequest the\nSDK traces by default, and the SDK does not recognise it in this app: no\nrequest had a span or a `sentry-trace` header, so every `GET /api/account`\nstarted a trace of its own on the .NET side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"24000bd8d0a0b909dc3718c74cf47873140edd88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/24000bd8d0a0b909dc3718c74cf47873140edd88"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc","verification":{"payload":"tree 24000bd8d0a0b909dc3718c74cf47873140edd88\nparent 6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790327707 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790327707 +0200\n\nMove the mobile app to @sentry/react-native 8, and trace its fetch by hand (#17)\n\n* Give the first native test enough time for a cold transform cache\n\nThe 30s limit from the previous commit passed on two CI runs and failed on\nthe third. The first test of each file is where babel transforms expo-router,\nReact Native and the screens, and CI always starts with an empty transform\ncache. Measured cold, that test takes 25s on a laptop and 30 to 45s on a CI\nrunner; warm, it takes 2.6s.\n\nThe limit is now 120s, so it covers a cold cache with room to spare and\nstill catches a test that truly hangs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Move the mobile app to @sentry/react-native 8, and trace its fetch by hand\n\nExpo 57 pins `~7.11.0`, and 7.x stamps spans from `performance.timeOrigin`,\nwhich on React Native 0.86 under iOS falls behind the wall clock by every hour\nthe machine has slept. A simulator left open for days sent navigation spans\ndated days in the past: Sentry accepted them with a 200, and no search for\n\"the last hour\" ever found them. 8.25 stopped trusting that clock\n(getsentry/sentry-react-native#6654). `expo.install.exclude` keeps\n`expo install --fix` from putting 7.x back.\n\n`traceFetch: true` is set by hand because the SDK''s own guess is wrong here.\nExpo 57''s global fetch is `expo/fetch`, which bypasses the XMLHttpRequest the\nSDK traces by default, and the SDK does not recognise it in this app: no\nrequest had a span or a `sentry-trace` header, so every `GET /api/account`\nstarted a trace of its own on the .NET side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtjubCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAhbsQABFPgqU+VXASco+I5G064I56\nn2ZRfQNTZhyPN5o+mbPApVZME+59ctJHdzAaXBEjneRao8rEGBNWIA8wGk+g7acy\n5N9YwNIrmmnyj2/srCvSQj+7zCkSFUFT1X9xf9BRuO66bUvMfiXJH5tCY7i5+YB2\noFQmRGlU9vXg1/Sac+kvIa7eyl0OjUJAKWmf3eW2njJJwQii7JwMgUmR3PpcLGFT\nzoMvII2ldJpNc8xSrNCPTi6XPensxWhKR+0aoyOsZ4eWTYiD3i/0MJqZmG9fli15\nVYpqtd/DWUGxltabnqdTSvHX7jtIq8jPC++4thqefjhz8KBGDM7jBwOJZrGe+TDE\nvjYuIYzoaHDgtB3eyswV8pPuy+iRByVguP5aQMmaEC+72u5R3yCerOi+cpQLyPwL\nsCls0aaTM+YrFA6KNCtR8rE3ryQf8owZm5F7CEjX8Azm1tVlxnfns2RJRTSjOiIC\n7vDNta+KMqpfI35HZgXsCnm6mBVXgnLMwSUR7yd5esG/7BYk6aoVJkjDQi100aAn\nBXbDq9dAGdaBkwOG5qsqHCw8zr2oSuWWKCWTY0KqLQAvJHQbhk/vsc6oo2lL85x8\nY9S946xo7W0Sfq7ONByMdYeGTZxhNyQNtVwZDF4TgiaJuvxXCY9sGpI9Tl0CbXg+\nmq3cOxikEgsIIe0PB1DE\n=EJmW\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T09:15:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKGRhNTViZGI1NGI3YWZiMTc1MTM0MDkwNDY0ZWFiYmNjZThiYjdjYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc","sha":"6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc"}],"sha":"da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/da55bdb54b7afb175134090464eabbcce8bb7cbc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T09:09:25Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T09:09:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Observe the app end to end, split it into services and remotes, and add a mobile app (#16)\n\n* Report what the browser throws, with a screen for the user and a stack for us\n\nA crash in the app was, until now, a blank document and a line in a console\nnobody is reading. This wires @sentry/react 11 to the Sentry project\nalexisregnaud/frontend-demo-app and puts something in front of both halves of\nthat: a fallback screen for whoever hit it, and an un-minified stack for\nwhoever has to fix it.\n\nThe router is passed to tanstackRouterBrowserTracingIntegration rather than\nletting the SDK read the URL, so a span is named by its route. With no\nparameterised route today the two look identical; the first one added is what\nsplits a comparable span into one per id, and naming them by route now is what\nprevents it.\n\nSentry stays off unless VITE_SENTRY_DSN is set. The integration tier runs a\nreal Chromium, so an unconditional init would send every failure a test\nprovokes on purpose - the API-is-down cases especially - to the project as if\nproduction had broken.\n\ndataCollection.userInfo is off explicitly. v11 replaced sendDefaultPii and\ninverted its default: v10 collected nothing personal unless told to, v11\ncollects unless told not to, and this app''s subject is somebody''s account\ndetails.\n\nAppErrorBoundary sits outside the router, which is the point - a crash in\nRootLayout or in the route tree has to land somewhere that needs neither still\nstanding. It also earns its keep over the global window.onerror handler the SDK\nalready installs: React hands the component stack to componentDidCatch and to\nnothing else, so without a boundary Sentry gets the error and no idea which\ncomponent was rendering.\n\nSource maps and their upload share one switch, SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN. Set, the\nbuild emits hidden maps, sends them to Sentry and deletes them from dist/;\nunset, none is generated, so a plain yarn build leaves nothing to leak. The\nplugin needs url: https://de.sentry.io - this organisation is in the EU region\nand an upload to the default host reaches nothing.\n\nThe new CI job runs yarn build, which nothing in CI did before: verify\ntype-checks the app but never bundles it, so a StyleX or React Compiler failure\nhad no gate. The token turns the same command into the source-map upload, and\nits absence on a fork''s pull request changes nothing but what the build emits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Put ASP.NET Core and EF Core behind the API, where the products actually are (ADR 0002)\n\nThe account API was Node on `--experimental-strip-types` over Drizzle; it is\nnow a .NET process: minimal APIs in `server/Api/`, Postgres through EF Core and\nNpgsql, migrations generated by `dotnet ef`, committed, and applied by the API\non start. ADR 0002 says why - the frontend this repository demonstrates faces\n.NET in production, and a Node backend was showing every rule about the\nboundary against a process that does not exist there.\n\nThe contract is untouched, which is what makes this a decision the frontend\nnever has to know about: `/api/account`, `/__test__/account`, `/health`, the\n`e2e-session` cookie and its header, the JSON shape, port 3001. Nothing under\n`src/` changes.\n\nWhat moved around it:\n\n- `yarn api:test` is new - xunit against a Postgres that Testcontainers starts\n  for the run, so it needs Docker and runs in the test job rather than under\n  `yarn test`. `yarn verify` ends on `dotnet build -warnaserror`, the same bar\n  `tsc` holds on the other side.\n- `DATABASE_URL` is now Npgsql''s `key=value` form; a `postgres://` URL is not\n  one it reads. CI and .env.example carry the new spelling.\n- `global.json` pins the SDK the way `.nvmrc` pins Node, and names the test\n  runner xunit.v3 needs. CI installs it with `setup-dotnet`.\n- Playwright waits 120s for the API instead of 30: `dotnet run` restores and\n  compiles before it listens.\n- `server/` leaves the tsconfig include and the Vitest node tier, both for the\n  same reason - there is no TypeScript there anymore.\n\nSentry.AspNetCore comes with it, reading `SENTRY_DSN` into the same\n`frontend-demo-api` project the Node version reported to, so the trace the\nbrowser starts still continues on this side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Catch what a route throws, and name the span by the route it threw in\n\nThe data router was catching route errors before the Sentry boundary outside it\ncould see them, so a crashed page rendered React Router''s own default screen and\nreached Sentry never - visible only in a production build, since React re-throws\nto `window.onerror` in development and hides the gap. `RouteErrorBoundary` is the\n`errorElement` that closes it: mounted below the layout so the navigation\nsurvives the crash, above the three pages so one boundary covers them all, and\nreporting by hand in an effect because the router took the error out of the path\nwhere the SDK''s global handler would have found it.\n\nThe screen the user sees is now `CrashScreen`, shared by both boundaries - the\none inside the router and `AppErrorBoundary` outside it - so the two cannot\ndrift into two different apologies. Each boundary keeps what is genuinely its\nown: the shell around it, and what \"Try again\" means (`resetError` outside,\na reload inside).\n\n`initSentry()` moves from main.tsx to routes.tsx, where it has to be:\n`wrapCreateBrowserRouter` runs at module scope and Sentry requires init before\nit, which a call in an entry point cannot promise - every import is evaluated\nbefore its first statement. The router integration comes from\n`@sentry/react/react-router` and replaces the TanStack Router one the app no\nlonger uses; with the wrapper, a navigation span is named `/account/edit` rather\nthan by the URL that was visited.\n\nTwo leaks found by measuring, not by reading:\n\n- The integration suite was reporting its deliberate failures as production\n  crashes. A DSN in .env is a DSN Vitest gets too, because Vite loads .env in\n  every mode - `Error: route exploded` landed in the project as\n  FRONTEND-DEMO-APP-5. `MODE === \"test\"` is the second condition that stops it.\n- `yarn build` uploaded no source maps with a token sitting in .env, silently:\n  Vite puts .env in `import.meta.env` for the client bundle and leaves\n  `process.env` alone, where the plugin reads it. The build script runs Vite\n  through `node --env-file-if-exists=.env` now.\n\n`e2e/home.spec.ts` matches its API calls on the origin as well as the path -\nSentry''s ingest endpoint is `POST /api/<projectId>/envelope/`, and with a DSN\nset the browser reporting the pageload was counted as a call the page made.\n\nA successful save logs the SHAPE of what was saved - which language, whether a\nphone was given - through `Sentry.logger`, so it reads inside the trace next to\nthe spans. Never the contents, which is the same line `dataCollection.userInfo`\ndraws.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Enable the frontend-skills plugin for everyone who opens this repository\n\n`.claude/settings.json` is the checked-in half of the settings, so the plugin\nthe standards live in is on by default rather than something each clone has to\nswitch on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Report the failures react-query was catching, and say who hit them, how long they took and how often\n\nFour gaps between \"Sentry is wired up\" and \"Sentry can answer a question\",\nclosed together because each one is a column in the issue list that reads empty\nwithout it.\n\nThe first is the one that lost events. react-query CATCHES what a `queryFn` or\na `mutationFn` throws and hands it back as `isError`, which is exactly what the\npages want - the account form shows a banner and offers to try again. The cost\nis that `window.onerror` never fires, because the error was handled, on purpose,\nby the UI. A 500 on /api/account was a visible failure that reached Sentry\nnever. `QueryCache`/`MutationCache` `onError` in src/query-client.ts is where it\nis reported now, on the caches rather than on each `useQuery`, so a vertical\nthat adds a query is covered without knowing that file exists. The query key\nrides along as a tag - one `Error: Request failed with status 500` looks like\nany other in the list, and the key is what says which fetch it was.\n\n`Sentry.setUser` gives the browser an id, because with nothing set the \"users\naffected\" column reads 0 for everything and an issue one person reloaded fifty\ntimes sorts the same as one fifty people hit once. A random uuid in\nlocalStorage, not a name and not the account the app is about - the same line\n`dataCollection.userInfo: false` draws. It counts and it groups, which is all\nthe column needs.\n\nProfiling is turned on for both halves, which is the sampled call stack under\nthe spans that already exist: the trace says the save took 400ms, the profile\nsays which functions spent them. In the browser that is\n`browserProfilingIntegration` on a `trace` lifecycle, so the profiler follows\n`tracesSampleRate` instead of recording the whole page - and it is silent,\nwith no error and no profile, unless the document was served under\n`Document-Policy: js-profiling`. vite.config.ts sends that header for both\nservers; a deployment has to set it on whatever serves index.html, and it is\nthe one piece of this setup that cannot be inherited from the repository. On\nthe API it is Sentry.Profiling, which ships apart from the SDK and is marked\nALPHA on .NET 8+; `ProfilesSampleRate` multiplies the existing sampler rather\nthan replacing it, so the /health polls Playwright makes are not profiled\neither.\n\n`SENTRY_RELEASE` is read in Program.cs so the two projects agree on a name.\nThe .NET SDK already stamps the commit into the assembly version, so its\ndefault reads `Api@1.0.0+8ffc29c` where the browser''s release - set by\n@sentry/vite-plugin - is the bare sha. Both right, spelled differently, and a\nsearch for one release found one half of the request. One\n`SENTRY_RELEASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` now covers both.\n\nThe save on the edit page gets a span of its own and two metrics. `account.save`\nopens when the user submits and closes when the cache has the server''s answer,\nwhich is the duration they actually waited - the `http.client` span the SDK\nadds measures the request, not the wait. It has to BE the `mutationFn`, since\n`startSpan` closes on the promise it is given and wrapping `mutate` would\nmeasure the queueing. The metrics are the same event the log already writes,\ncounted rather than written down: `account.updated` with the language as an\nATTRIBUTE, so it stays one series to chart instead of one metric per language,\nand a distribution for the bio length, where the interesting question is p50\nagainst p95 rather than the total.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split the frontend into a shell and one remote per vertical, federated at runtime (ADR 0003)\n\nThe app is now three Vite builds over one source tree, wired with\n@module-federation/vite. The shell keeps index.html, the layout, the router,\nthe providers and the Sentry client; each vertical exposes its pages through\nsrc/<name>/pages.ts, is built and served on its own port, and is fetched by\nthe shell when a route first needs it. The URL is the whole contract:\nsrc/routes.tsx says where a page lives and loads it with React Router''s\n`lazy`, and a remote that fails to load lands in RouteErrorBoundary with the\nnavigation still standing.\n\nfederation.config.ts names the remotes, their ports and the packages the\nthree builds must hold one copy of (react, react-dom, react-router,\nreact-query, @sentry/react). vite.base.ts holds the toolchain they share;\neach build collects only its own StyleX stylesheet, the tokens included.\n\nTwo things the switch surfaced:\n\n- A data router clears a `lazy` loader on the route object once it has run,\n  so a second router over the same array answers \"No route matches URL /\".\n  `routes` is a factory now, one tree per router.\n- Three dev servers of one root share node_modules/.vite and overwrite each\n  other''s pre-bundles. Each build has its own cacheDir, and a remote''s\n  dependency scan starts from what it exposes rather than the shell''s html.\n\nThe integration tier resolves `account/pages` from source (alias.ts), so it\nnever crosses the wire; the e2e tier starts the API, the shell and both\nremotes and is the one that proves the federation. `yarn dev`, `build` and\n`preview` drive all three; `build:<name>` and `preview:<name>` drive one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say whose account this is on the welcome, with a component the home remote fetches from the account remote\n\nThe welcome embeds AccountPreview - the account in one line, name and email -\nand that component belongs to the account vertical: the query, the field\nnames and the loading and error states are all its own. Home reaches it as\n`account/preview`, a second module the account remote exposes beside its\npages, behind React.lazy, Suspense and an error boundary of the page''s own,\nso an account deployment that is down takes the preview and nothing else.\n\nThis is the one place a vertical consumes another, and it is here to show\nthe cost: vite.home.config.ts now declares `consumes: [\"account\"]`, and the\nhome build depends on the account deployment. `remoteConfig` takes that\nlist, `remoteRef` in federation.config.ts serves the shell and the remotes\nalike, and the vertical stylesheets are named styles.css since more than one\nexposed module imports them.\n\nThe home integration test asserts the name and email in the preview, and\nthat a failed query leaves the welcome standing. The e2e spec, which used\nto prove the welcome fetched nothing, now proves it fetched exactly the\naccount - and that the preview came over the wire from the account remote''s\nown origin, which no other tier can show.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Send each remote''s errors to a Sentry project of its own, chosen by the build that threw\n\nThe app is three builds (ADR 0003) but one Sentry client, initialised once\nin the shell: a second init, or a second copy of the SDK, would leave the\nremotes'' spans reporting to nothing. So what tells the builds apart is the\ntransport. Each build''s Vite plugin now stamps its bundles with the DSN and\nrelease of that build''s project (`moduleMetadata`), `moduleMetadataIntegration`\ncopies the stamp onto every frame that came from such a bundle, and\n`makeMultiplexedTransport` sends the error to the project the stamp names.\nA crash inside the account''s preview is the account team''s, whether the\naccount page or the home page rendered it.\n\nThe owner is decided in `beforeSend`, from the DEEPEST stamped frame - the\none closest to the throw - and not in the transport''s matcher: the\nintegration strips the stamps as the envelope is built, so a matcher reading\nframes finds nothing. Measured; every crash then went to the default project.\nsrc/sentry-owner.ts holds that reading as a pure function with a unit test\nover an event literal, and ../sentry.ts wires it in.\n\nA remote gets its own project by setting `SENTRY_DSN_<NAME>` - in .env for a\nlocal build, as a secret for CI. Unset, its build reports into the shell''s\nproject like before, so nothing has to exist in Sentry before a remote can\nbuild. Traces are not routed: one crosses every build and stays in the app''s\nproject.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show the line a .NET frame threw on in Sentry, by embedding the sources in the PDB the Release build uploads\n\nA browser frame in Sentry shows the code around it because the Vite plugin\nuploads source maps. A .NET frame showed a method name and nothing else: the\nAPI build handed Sentry no debug files at all. It now does the equivalent -\nin Release, `EmbedAllSources` writes the source text into the portable PDB,\nand `SentryUploadSymbols` sends that PDB through the sentry-cli the Sentry\npackage ships. Sentry extracts the sources it needs and shows the frame in\ncontext, with or without the PDB beside the deployed DLL.\n\nNot `SentryUploadSources`, the documented alternative, which silently does\nnothing here. The .NET SDK puts a Source Link map into every PDB, and\nsentry-cli treats a file that has a Source Link the same as one whose text\nis embedded: it skips it. Every file has one, so it bundles nothing, prints\n\"Resolved source code for 0 debug information files\" and succeeds. Sentry\ndoes not follow the link either (symbolic #735). Api.csproj records this so\nnobody flips the switch back.\n\nThe upload hangs off the same `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` as the browser''s maps and\nhappens only in Release, so `yarn verify`, `yarn api:test` and `dotnet run`\nnever upload. `yarn api:build` is that Release build, and the CI build job\nruns it after `yarn build`. The token has to be the organization kind\n(`sntrys_...`), which is what the org''s token page issues; .env.example says\nso. Verified against a real crash: the issue in frontend-demo-api now shows\nthe `throw` line with three lines of context on either side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start a navigation span at the click, so the remote it fetched on the way shows inside it\n\nA navigation into /account read in Sentry as a bare API call: none of the\naccount remote''s chunks appeared in the span, while the pageload of / showed\nevery one of the home remote''s. Measured, the span started 96ms after the\nclick, and the whole of that gap was the remote being fetched. A data router\nresolves a route''s `lazy` before it commits the URL, and the URL change is\nwhat Sentry''s `wrapCreateBrowserRouter` listens for; by the time the span\nexisted, every chunk had landed, and Sentry drops a resource entry that\npredates the span it would belong to.\n\nThe router''s own `instrumentations` hook sees the click. `routerInstrumentation`\nrecords when `navigate()` was called, and `beforeStartSpan` on the tracing\nintegration hands that time to the navigation span as its `startTime`, then\nclears it. Only navigation spans - a pageload starts with the document. The\nback button goes through `popstate`, never reaches the hook, and has no\nremote to fetch, so its span loses nothing by starting at the URL change.\n\nA module variable carries the time because at the moment the router calls\nthe hook there is no span to hold it - that is the gap itself. React Router''s\n`route.instrument({ lazy })` would have been the natural place to time the\nload, and it does not fire here: the router wraps only the function form of\n`lazy`, and routes.tsx uses the object form on purpose.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split the backend into two services, so a save reads as one trace across both (ADR 0004)\n\nEverything this repository showed about tracing crossed exactly one boundary,\nthe browser to the API, and one boundary is the case that works by accident.\nThe question it could not answer was whether a request still reads as one\nthing once the backend is more than one process.\n\nIt does. server/Notifications is a second ASP.NET Core service on 3002, which\nthe account API calls over HTTP when an account changes. Measured against a\nreal save, Sentry received seven spans under one trace id across two projects:\n`PUT /api/account` at 140ms in frontend-demo-api, its `db.query` spans, an\n`http.client` span of 87ms to 3002, and in frontend-demo-notifications the\n`POST /notifications/account-changed` transaction with a `notification.send`\nspan inside it.\n\nThree things do that work and none of them propagates anything by hand.\nSentry.AspNetCore continues the trace named by the `sentry-trace` and `baggage`\nheaders on an incoming request. `AddHttpClient<NotificationsClient>` turns on\nthe HTTP client factory, which Sentry hangs a handler filter on, so outgoing\nrequests carry the same headers and open an `http.client` span - a\n`new HttpClient()` would reach the service just as well and show two unrelated\ntraces. And one shared `TracesSampler` honours `IsParentSampled` before\nanything else, so a trace the browser chose to keep is kept at every hop\nrather than recorded at one and dropped at the next.\n\nThe two services share no domain type. `AccountChanged` is declared on both\nsides and the projects do not reference each other, because a shared record is\na shared assembly and a shared assembly is two services that deploy together.\nserver/Observability is the one project both reference, and it earns that by\ncarrying the Sentry setup and nothing that knows what an account is.\n\nA notification cannot fail a save: the account is already written by the time\nthe call runs, so NotificationsClient swallows every exception and logs a\nwarning. Verified with the service down - the PUT still returns 200, and the\nfailed `http.client` span inside a successful transaction is what keeps the\nfault visible. The call is awaited rather than queued, which is what keeps\nthat span inside the transaction and lets e2e/account.spec.ts read the\nnotification back out of the other process the moment the save returns.\n\nThe frontend is unchanged: no new origin, no proxy entry, no CORS.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Rename server/Api to server/Accounts, so both services are named after their subject\n\n`src/` groups by subject and AGENTS.md says so; `server/` did not, and with\ntwo services the inconsistency showed. `Notifications` named a subject, `Api`\nnamed a layer, and \"the API\" stopped being a distinguishing word the moment\nthe notifications service was one too - the comments in that folder already\nsaid \"the account API\" throughout and never \"the Api\".\n\nPlural, because the folder holds a record called `Account`. With the root\nnamespace renamed, `Account.Account` is legal C# and unpleasant, while\n`Accounts.Account` reads fine and matches the `accounts` table and the\n`AccountsDb` context already there. The EF migrations move with it: their\nnamespace is what `dotnet ef` would generate next, so the committed files and\nthe next generated one agree.\n\nThe scripts moved with the folder, or renaming it would only have moved the\ninconsistency. `accounts:start` addresses the service; `server:test` and\n`server:build` address the solution, which is what they always did - both run\nover server.slnx and have covered the notifications service since it existed.\n\nThree names deliberately left alone. The Sentry project stays\n`frontend-demo-api`: renaming a project breaks saved queries and alerts for a\ncosmetic gain, and the DSN is unchanged either way. ADR 0002 still says\n`server/Api/`, which was true the day it was written, and ADR 0004 now records\nwhere it went. docs/intentional-tests/ are dated run logs that already carry\nthe older `server/api.ts` from before the .NET move; they are records of a run,\nnot documentation of a layout.\n\n`yarn verify`, `yarn test`, `yarn server:test` and `yarn e2e` all green after\nthe move.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Rewrite the notifications service in Node, so one trace crosses two runtimes (ADR 0005)\n\nSame port, same three routes, same wire contract - `node:http` and TypeScript\nrun through Node''s type stripping, instrumented by @sentry/node. The account API\nis untouched, NotificationsClient.cs included, which is the cheapest possible\ndemonstration that ADR 0004 drew the boundary in the right place.\n\nADR 0004 split the backend to ask whether the observability story survives more\nthan one service, but both services were ASP.NET Core - the case most likely to\nwork by accident. A trace is linked by its id: not by its project, which ADR\n0004 showed, and now not by its runtime either.\n\nserver/Observability/ goes with it. It existed to hold the Sentry setup both\nservices shared; with one .NET service left, a library with one consumer is not\na boundary, so SentrySetup.cs moves in beside Program.cs.\n\n* Pass the commit sha as SENTRY_RELEASE, so every part names one build\n\nA release is global to the organisation and carries events from every project\nunder it, so one sha is what lets a deploy be compared across the five. The Vite\nplugin would derive the same value from git on its own; naming it in the build\njob is what makes the three frontend builds and the two backends agree on a\nstring rather than each deriving one.\n\nThat reaches the browser only - Vite stamps it into each bundle at build time.\nThe two backend services read it from their own environment at startup, and they\ndo not degrade the same way without it: the account API falls back to\n`Accounts@1.0.0+<sha>` from its assembly version, the Node service reports no\nrelease at all. .env.example says so, and says why a deployment should carry the\nsha into each artifact rather than set it from the platform''s own config.\n\nIt also records what the move to Node did to source context: that service needs\nno auth token and no maps, because it runs the TypeScript it shipped and\n@sentry/node reads the file off the disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Move the design tokens into packages/tokens, so the workspace split has a leaf to start from\n\nFirst step of the yarn workspaces + Turborepo migration. The tokens belong to\nno vertical and every build has to compile the same file, which makes them the\nsmallest package that proves the split works.\n\nThe package exports `./tokens.stylex`, not `.`, and the imports read\n`@demo/tokens/tokens.stylex`. That is not decoration: the StyleX babel plugin\ntests the IMPORT SPECIFIER for the `.stylex` suffix before it resolves\nanything, so `@demo/tokens` is rejected outright - \"Could not resolve the path\nto the imported file\" - even though Node resolves the package fine. StyleX''s\nown `aliases` option cannot help, because the suffix check runs first.\n\nThe generated CSS variable names change once, because the babel plugin hashes\nthe file path relative to `rootDir` and the file moved. What matters is that\nthe three builds still agree: shell, account and home each emit the same 26\nvariables, which is the invariant `unstable_moduleResolution` exists to\nprotect. `stylex.config.ts` stays at the repository root so all three keep one\n`rootDir`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Take the route tree and the handlers as arguments, so the test harness stops depending on the app\n\nsrc/testing/ is about to become a package, and it could not: it imported\n../routes, ../query-client and @account/mocks/handlers, while the tests of\naccount, home AND layout all import it back. One source tree hides that; two\npackages make it a cycle, and Turborepo rejects a cyclic task graph.\n\nThe harness now takes what it mounts:\n\n- `renderRoute(path, app)` and `renderComponent(ui, app)` receive an\n  `AppUnderTest` - the route factory and the QueryClient factory - instead of\n  importing them. src/app-under-test.ts is the one place the shell names its\n  own pair, and a vertical''s test passes it.\n- `createWorker(handlers)` replaces the module-level `setupWorker(...handlers)`.\n  vitest.setup.ts creates it, being outside every package and therefore the\n  place the harness and the account''s mocks can meet. A test that overrides a\n  response now reads `worker().use(...)`; the handlers still go to\n  `setupWorker`, so `resetHandlers()` returns to the same list as before.\n\nsrc/testing/ now imports react, react-router, react-query, msw and\nvitest-browser-react, and nothing of this app''s.\n\nThe edge this makes visible was always real: a vertical''s integration test\nmounts the SHELL''s route tree, deliberately, so a renamed path fails in the\ntest rather than in production. It is now written at the call site.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Turborepo over the scripts that exist, so the three builds stop running twice\n\nNo file moves: this wires turbo to today''s root scripts and proves the cache\nbefore anything is split.\n\nThe measured result, on this tree: `build` goes from 17.6s to 5ms on a second\nrun and restores dist/ from the cache, which is the duplication ci.yml pays\nfor today - the `build` job and the `e2e` job each run the same three Vite\nbuilds. A real edit to a source file misses, and reverting it hits again.\n\n`.turbo/` had to be gitignored for any of that to work. A root task''s default\ninputs are the whole root package, so the cache directory was hashing itself:\nevery run wrote into it, which changed the hash, which guaranteed the next run\nmissed. The two .NET tasks get explicit `inputs` for the same reason - without\nthem any frontend edit would rebuild the server.\n\n`devEngines.packageManager` is required by turbo to resolve the workspace. It\nalso makes npm refuse to run in this repo, which is correct and costs nothing:\nnothing here invokes npx.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Extract packages/testing, so the harness is a package every vertical can depend on\n\nThe third namespace of alias.ts is gone: `@demo/testing/render-route` is a real\npackage name that yarn''s workspace symlink resolves, so neither alias.ts nor\ntsconfig.json `paths` has to spell it out. Two namespaces left, and this file\ngoes with the last one.\n\npackages/query-client, which the plan called for, turns out not to be needed.\nInjecting the QueryClient factory through AppUnderTest already removed the\nharness''s reason to reach for it, and the shell is now its only consumer - so\nit stays where it is. One package fewer than planned.\n\nThe harness is declared in the ROOT devDependencies rather than in each\nvertical''s. That is deliberate and not laziness: layout''s tests import the\naccount''s mocks while the account''s tests mount the shell''s routes, so any\napp-to-app test dependency would be a cycle, and turbo traverses\ndevDependencies. `test` is a root task with no topology, and the root package\nis outside the apps'' graph, so this is where the test tier composes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split src/ into apps/shell, apps/account and apps/home, and delete alias.ts\n\nThe three verticals move together rather than one per commit, because the test\ntier entangles them: the layout''s tests use the account''s mocks while the\naccount''s tests mount the shell''s routes. Split one at a time, each commit\nwould have carried a `../../../../src/...` import waiting for the next one.\n\nalias.ts is gone, and with it the `paths` block in tsconfig.json. The\nduplication the file''s own header called \"the one thing to keep in sync\" is\nnot replaced by anything: `@demo/account/mocks/db-utils` is a package name,\nwhich yarn resolves through node_modules and TypeScript reads out of that\npackage''s `exports`. Playwright, which had no alias config and read the\nmapping straight from tsconfig.json, now resolves the way everything else\ndoes. What each package exposes is its `exports` field - a real surface, where\nbefore every file of every vertical was reachable from everywhere.\n\n`remotesFromSource` survives, moved into vitest.config.ts, which was always\nits only consumer. It is also why the packages are SCOPED: a package literally\nnamed `account` would make `import(\"account/pages\")` resolve as a real\nsubpath in the shell''s build and silently bundle what must be federated - the\nsame failure alias.ts guarded against, with nothing left to remove.\n\nTwo things had to change for the builds to survive their new cwd:\n\n- StyleX''s postcss half globs against `process.cwd()` unless the pattern is\n  absolute, and each app now builds with its own directory as cwd. A relative\n  `packages/tokens/**` would have matched nothing and emitted a stylesheet\n  with no rules - no error, just an unstyled page. `fromRoot()` in\n  stylex.config.ts anchors every pattern at the repository root.\n- Each app writes into its OWN dist/. Turbo restores a cache entry by\n  overwriting the task''s declared outputs, so three builds sharing one\n  directory would have had one restore clobber another''s artefacts.\n\nMeasured after the split: 66/73/33 CSS rules per build and the same 26 token\nvariables in all three, identical to before it; three builds in parallel\ninstead of three in series; and `turbo run build` twice is FULL TURBO. The e2e\nsuite passes, which is the tier that proves home still fetches the account''s\npreview over the wire rather than bundling it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Cache turbo in CI and write down what the split decided (ADR 0006)\n\nThe `build` and `e2e` jobs carry .turbo between runs, keyed per job. Per job\non purpose: `build` runs with the Sentry secrets and `e2e` without them, those\nvariables are in the task''s `env`, so the two hash differently and must not\nshare an entry - a token-bearing build served from a token-less one would\nupload nothing and say nothing. The win is a commit that touches only server/,\ndocs/ or evals/, where both jobs restore the three builds instead of running\nthem.\n\nADR 0006 records the decision and the four things about it that are invisible\nin the code: why the packages must stay scoped, why the tokens are imported as\n`@demo/tokens/tokens.stylex` and no other spelling, why StyleX''s postcss globs\nare absolute, and why the test tier composes at the root rather than between\napps. It also corrects what the first commit of this series claimed: the\nStyleX plugin hashes a `defineVars` file by the nearest package.json above it,\nnot by `rootDir`, which is only a fallback when there is no package.json at\nall. Same observed effect, different mechanism - and it means the tokens are\nnow stable by construction rather than by a config value.\n\nAGENTS.md''s Verticals section is rewritten for packages; the Tests section is\nuntouched, because the contract it states - the FILENAME, not the folder,\ndecides a test''s tier - survived the migration intact.\n\nserver/Notifications/ stays where it is rather than becoming a workspace. It\nis a Node process with no build, its sibling is .NET and cannot move, and\ndocs/adr/0005 put the two together because they are one subject seen from two\nruntimes. Moving one for symmetry with apps/ would split that pair for nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Declare the test-tier dependencies off the root package, so an edit stops invalidating every build\n\nListing the apps in the ROOT devDependencies - which the previous commit did\nto avoid an app-to-app cycle - silently cost the cache the whole migration was\nfor. An internal dependency of the root package feeds turbo''s GLOBAL hash, so\n`hashOfInternalDependencies` moved on any workspace change and every task in\nthe repository missed. Editing one line of apps/account rebuilt all three\napps.\n\nMeasured before and after, on the same one-line edit to AccountPage.tsx:\n\n  before   @demo/account  MISS   @demo/shell  MISS   @demo/home  MISS\n  after    @demo/account  MISS   @demo/shell  HIT    @demo/home  HIT\n\n`@demo/testing` moves to each app''s own devDependencies, where it belongs: the\nharness is a leaf, so that edge is acyclic and scoped to the app that uses it.\nThe app-to-app edges - the layout''s tests reaching for the account''s mocks,\nthe account''s tests mounting the shell''s routes - stay undeclared. Yarn\nsymlinks every workspace member into the root node_modules whether or not\nanything declares it, so they resolve regardless; ADR 0006 now records that as\na known gap rather than leaving it to be rediscovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add apps/mobile as one Expo app, over a packages/account-core shared with the web\n\nThe account''s platform-free half - the Zod schema, the French-phone parser,\nthe API client, the useAccount query and the MSW handlers - moves out of\napps/account into packages/account-core, a leaf like packages/testing and\npackages/tokens. Both apps/account and apps/mobile import it; nothing else is\nshared. The one piece that had a platform in it, `window.location.origin` as\nthe API origin, becomes `setApiBaseUrl`, called by the host that has no window.\n\napps/mobile is a single Expo app (Expo Router, Unistyles, @sentry/react-native)\nrather than three remotes, because a binary has no independent deployment for\nModule Federation to buy. ADR 0007 records that, and what is deliberately\nwritten twice: the tokens (packages/tokens/tokens.native.ts beside the StyleX\nvars), the Sentry setup and the QueryClient.\n\nWiring around it:\n\n- `yarn test:native` runs the jest-expo tier against the same MSW handlers,\n  and CI runs it beside `yarn server:test`.\n- `yarn verify` type-checks apps/mobile, which the root tsconfig excludes.\n- turbo''s build forwards EXPO_PUBLIC_*, since Expo inlines them into the\n  bundle; the mobile dev script is `start`, not `dev`, so the web fan-out does\n  not bring Metro up.\n- @babel/core goes back to 7, which is what the Expo toolchain runs on.\n- The web Sentry projects are renamed demo-web-<remote>-frontend, to sit\n  beside the mobile app''s own project.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start the API without a DSN, and give the first native test time to load the app\n\nBoth failures only show up where nothing is configured, which is why they\npassed locally and failed on every CI run.\n\nThe account API threw at startup when SENTRY_DSN was unset: \"You must supply\na DSN to use Sentry\". SentrySetup.cs said Sentry was off unless the variable\nwas set, but this SDK treats a null DSN as a mistake, not as off. Locally .env\nalways sets one. In CI nothing does, so the e2e job''s web server never came\nup. An empty string is how the SDK is told to be off.\n\nThe first test in each native file timed out at jest''s default 5s. It is the\none that pays for loading expo-router, the screens and the mock network,\nwhich takes 2.6s on a laptop and more than 5s on a CI runner running three\nfiles at once. The rest of each file passed in milliseconds.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d6fdeecdfa0b9c19e7a0426e058e16d6ddd452b2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/d6fdeecdfa0b9c19e7a0426e058e16d6ddd452b2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc","verification":{"payload":"tree d6fdeecdfa0b9c19e7a0426e058e16d6ddd452b2\nparent d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790327365 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790327365 +0200\n\nObserve the app end to end, split it into services and remotes, and add a mobile app (#16)\n\n* Report what the browser throws, with a screen for the user and a stack for us\n\nA crash in the app was, until now, a blank document and a line in a console\nnobody is reading. This wires @sentry/react 11 to the Sentry project\nalexisregnaud/frontend-demo-app and puts something in front of both halves of\nthat: a fallback screen for whoever hit it, and an un-minified stack for\nwhoever has to fix it.\n\nThe router is passed to tanstackRouterBrowserTracingIntegration rather than\nletting the SDK read the URL, so a span is named by its route. With no\nparameterised route today the two look identical; the first one added is what\nsplits a comparable span into one per id, and naming them by route now is what\nprevents it.\n\nSentry stays off unless VITE_SENTRY_DSN is set. The integration tier runs a\nreal Chromium, so an unconditional init would send every failure a test\nprovokes on purpose - the API-is-down cases especially - to the project as if\nproduction had broken.\n\ndataCollection.userInfo is off explicitly. v11 replaced sendDefaultPii and\ninverted its default: v10 collected nothing personal unless told to, v11\ncollects unless told not to, and this app''s subject is somebody''s account\ndetails.\n\nAppErrorBoundary sits outside the router, which is the point - a crash in\nRootLayout or in the route tree has to land somewhere that needs neither still\nstanding. It also earns its keep over the global window.onerror handler the SDK\nalready installs: React hands the component stack to componentDidCatch and to\nnothing else, so without a boundary Sentry gets the error and no idea which\ncomponent was rendering.\n\nSource maps and their upload share one switch, SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN. Set, the\nbuild emits hidden maps, sends them to Sentry and deletes them from dist/;\nunset, none is generated, so a plain yarn build leaves nothing to leak. The\nplugin needs url: https://de.sentry.io - this organisation is in the EU region\nand an upload to the default host reaches nothing.\n\nThe new CI job runs yarn build, which nothing in CI did before: verify\ntype-checks the app but never bundles it, so a StyleX or React Compiler failure\nhad no gate. The token turns the same command into the source-map upload, and\nits absence on a fork''s pull request changes nothing but what the build emits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Put ASP.NET Core and EF Core behind the API, where the products actually are (ADR 0002)\n\nThe account API was Node on `--experimental-strip-types` over Drizzle; it is\nnow a .NET process: minimal APIs in `server/Api/`, Postgres through EF Core and\nNpgsql, migrations generated by `dotnet ef`, committed, and applied by the API\non start. ADR 0002 says why - the frontend this repository demonstrates faces\n.NET in production, and a Node backend was showing every rule about the\nboundary against a process that does not exist there.\n\nThe contract is untouched, which is what makes this a decision the frontend\nnever has to know about: `/api/account`, `/__test__/account`, `/health`, the\n`e2e-session` cookie and its header, the JSON shape, port 3001. Nothing under\n`src/` changes.\n\nWhat moved around it:\n\n- `yarn api:test` is new - xunit against a Postgres that Testcontainers starts\n  for the run, so it needs Docker and runs in the test job rather than under\n  `yarn test`. `yarn verify` ends on `dotnet build -warnaserror`, the same bar\n  `tsc` holds on the other side.\n- `DATABASE_URL` is now Npgsql''s `key=value` form; a `postgres://` URL is not\n  one it reads. CI and .env.example carry the new spelling.\n- `global.json` pins the SDK the way `.nvmrc` pins Node, and names the test\n  runner xunit.v3 needs. CI installs it with `setup-dotnet`.\n- Playwright waits 120s for the API instead of 30: `dotnet run` restores and\n  compiles before it listens.\n- `server/` leaves the tsconfig include and the Vitest node tier, both for the\n  same reason - there is no TypeScript there anymore.\n\nSentry.AspNetCore comes with it, reading `SENTRY_DSN` into the same\n`frontend-demo-api` project the Node version reported to, so the trace the\nbrowser starts still continues on this side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Catch what a route throws, and name the span by the route it threw in\n\nThe data router was catching route errors before the Sentry boundary outside it\ncould see them, so a crashed page rendered React Router''s own default screen and\nreached Sentry never - visible only in a production build, since React re-throws\nto `window.onerror` in development and hides the gap. `RouteErrorBoundary` is the\n`errorElement` that closes it: mounted below the layout so the navigation\nsurvives the crash, above the three pages so one boundary covers them all, and\nreporting by hand in an effect because the router took the error out of the path\nwhere the SDK''s global handler would have found it.\n\nThe screen the user sees is now `CrashScreen`, shared by both boundaries - the\none inside the router and `AppErrorBoundary` outside it - so the two cannot\ndrift into two different apologies. Each boundary keeps what is genuinely its\nown: the shell around it, and what \"Try again\" means (`resetError` outside,\na reload inside).\n\n`initSentry()` moves from main.tsx to routes.tsx, where it has to be:\n`wrapCreateBrowserRouter` runs at module scope and Sentry requires init before\nit, which a call in an entry point cannot promise - every import is evaluated\nbefore its first statement. The router integration comes from\n`@sentry/react/react-router` and replaces the TanStack Router one the app no\nlonger uses; with the wrapper, a navigation span is named `/account/edit` rather\nthan by the URL that was visited.\n\nTwo leaks found by measuring, not by reading:\n\n- The integration suite was reporting its deliberate failures as production\n  crashes. A DSN in .env is a DSN Vitest gets too, because Vite loads .env in\n  every mode - `Error: route exploded` landed in the project as\n  FRONTEND-DEMO-APP-5. `MODE === \"test\"` is the second condition that stops it.\n- `yarn build` uploaded no source maps with a token sitting in .env, silently:\n  Vite puts .env in `import.meta.env` for the client bundle and leaves\n  `process.env` alone, where the plugin reads it. The build script runs Vite\n  through `node --env-file-if-exists=.env` now.\n\n`e2e/home.spec.ts` matches its API calls on the origin as well as the path -\nSentry''s ingest endpoint is `POST /api/<projectId>/envelope/`, and with a DSN\nset the browser reporting the pageload was counted as a call the page made.\n\nA successful save logs the SHAPE of what was saved - which language, whether a\nphone was given - through `Sentry.logger`, so it reads inside the trace next to\nthe spans. Never the contents, which is the same line `dataCollection.userInfo`\ndraws.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Enable the frontend-skills plugin for everyone who opens this repository\n\n`.claude/settings.json` is the checked-in half of the settings, so the plugin\nthe standards live in is on by default rather than something each clone has to\nswitch on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Report the failures react-query was catching, and say who hit them, how long they took and how often\n\nFour gaps between \"Sentry is wired up\" and \"Sentry can answer a question\",\nclosed together because each one is a column in the issue list that reads empty\nwithout it.\n\nThe first is the one that lost events. react-query CATCHES what a `queryFn` or\na `mutationFn` throws and hands it back as `isError`, which is exactly what the\npages want - the account form shows a banner and offers to try again. The cost\nis that `window.onerror` never fires, because the error was handled, on purpose,\nby the UI. A 500 on /api/account was a visible failure that reached Sentry\nnever. `QueryCache`/`MutationCache` `onError` in src/query-client.ts is where it\nis reported now, on the caches rather than on each `useQuery`, so a vertical\nthat adds a query is covered without knowing that file exists. The query key\nrides along as a tag - one `Error: Request failed with status 500` looks like\nany other in the list, and the key is what says which fetch it was.\n\n`Sentry.setUser` gives the browser an id, because with nothing set the \"users\naffected\" column reads 0 for everything and an issue one person reloaded fifty\ntimes sorts the same as one fifty people hit once. A random uuid in\nlocalStorage, not a name and not the account the app is about - the same line\n`dataCollection.userInfo: false` draws. It counts and it groups, which is all\nthe column needs.\n\nProfiling is turned on for both halves, which is the sampled call stack under\nthe spans that already exist: the trace says the save took 400ms, the profile\nsays which functions spent them. In the browser that is\n`browserProfilingIntegration` on a `trace` lifecycle, so the profiler follows\n`tracesSampleRate` instead of recording the whole page - and it is silent,\nwith no error and no profile, unless the document was served under\n`Document-Policy: js-profiling`. vite.config.ts sends that header for both\nservers; a deployment has to set it on whatever serves index.html, and it is\nthe one piece of this setup that cannot be inherited from the repository. On\nthe API it is Sentry.Profiling, which ships apart from the SDK and is marked\nALPHA on .NET 8+; `ProfilesSampleRate` multiplies the existing sampler rather\nthan replacing it, so the /health polls Playwright makes are not profiled\neither.\n\n`SENTRY_RELEASE` is read in Program.cs so the two projects agree on a name.\nThe .NET SDK already stamps the commit into the assembly version, so its\ndefault reads `Api@1.0.0+8ffc29c` where the browser''s release - set by\n@sentry/vite-plugin - is the bare sha. Both right, spelled differently, and a\nsearch for one release found one half of the request. One\n`SENTRY_RELEASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` now covers both.\n\nThe save on the edit page gets a span of its own and two metrics. `account.save`\nopens when the user submits and closes when the cache has the server''s answer,\nwhich is the duration they actually waited - the `http.client` span the SDK\nadds measures the request, not the wait. It has to BE the `mutationFn`, since\n`startSpan` closes on the promise it is given and wrapping `mutate` would\nmeasure the queueing. The metrics are the same event the log already writes,\ncounted rather than written down: `account.updated` with the language as an\nATTRIBUTE, so it stays one series to chart instead of one metric per language,\nand a distribution for the bio length, where the interesting question is p50\nagainst p95 rather than the total.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split the frontend into a shell and one remote per vertical, federated at runtime (ADR 0003)\n\nThe app is now three Vite builds over one source tree, wired with\n@module-federation/vite. The shell keeps index.html, the layout, the router,\nthe providers and the Sentry client; each vertical exposes its pages through\nsrc/<name>/pages.ts, is built and served on its own port, and is fetched by\nthe shell when a route first needs it. The URL is the whole contract:\nsrc/routes.tsx says where a page lives and loads it with React Router''s\n`lazy`, and a remote that fails to load lands in RouteErrorBoundary with the\nnavigation still standing.\n\nfederation.config.ts names the remotes, their ports and the packages the\nthree builds must hold one copy of (react, react-dom, react-router,\nreact-query, @sentry/react). vite.base.ts holds the toolchain they share;\neach build collects only its own StyleX stylesheet, the tokens included.\n\nTwo things the switch surfaced:\n\n- A data router clears a `lazy` loader on the route object once it has run,\n  so a second router over the same array answers \"No route matches URL /\".\n  `routes` is a factory now, one tree per router.\n- Three dev servers of one root share node_modules/.vite and overwrite each\n  other''s pre-bundles. Each build has its own cacheDir, and a remote''s\n  dependency scan starts from what it exposes rather than the shell''s html.\n\nThe integration tier resolves `account/pages` from source (alias.ts), so it\nnever crosses the wire; the e2e tier starts the API, the shell and both\nremotes and is the one that proves the federation. `yarn dev`, `build` and\n`preview` drive all three; `build:<name>` and `preview:<name>` drive one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say whose account this is on the welcome, with a component the home remote fetches from the account remote\n\nThe welcome embeds AccountPreview - the account in one line, name and email -\nand that component belongs to the account vertical: the query, the field\nnames and the loading and error states are all its own. Home reaches it as\n`account/preview`, a second module the account remote exposes beside its\npages, behind React.lazy, Suspense and an error boundary of the page''s own,\nso an account deployment that is down takes the preview and nothing else.\n\nThis is the one place a vertical consumes another, and it is here to show\nthe cost: vite.home.config.ts now declares `consumes: [\"account\"]`, and the\nhome build depends on the account deployment. `remoteConfig` takes that\nlist, `remoteRef` in federation.config.ts serves the shell and the remotes\nalike, and the vertical stylesheets are named styles.css since more than one\nexposed module imports them.\n\nThe home integration test asserts the name and email in the preview, and\nthat a failed query leaves the welcome standing. The e2e spec, which used\nto prove the welcome fetched nothing, now proves it fetched exactly the\naccount - and that the preview came over the wire from the account remote''s\nown origin, which no other tier can show.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Send each remote''s errors to a Sentry project of its own, chosen by the build that threw\n\nThe app is three builds (ADR 0003) but one Sentry client, initialised once\nin the shell: a second init, or a second copy of the SDK, would leave the\nremotes'' spans reporting to nothing. So what tells the builds apart is the\ntransport. Each build''s Vite plugin now stamps its bundles with the DSN and\nrelease of that build''s project (`moduleMetadata`), `moduleMetadataIntegration`\ncopies the stamp onto every frame that came from such a bundle, and\n`makeMultiplexedTransport` sends the error to the project the stamp names.\nA crash inside the account''s preview is the account team''s, whether the\naccount page or the home page rendered it.\n\nThe owner is decided in `beforeSend`, from the DEEPEST stamped frame - the\none closest to the throw - and not in the transport''s matcher: the\nintegration strips the stamps as the envelope is built, so a matcher reading\nframes finds nothing. Measured; every crash then went to the default project.\nsrc/sentry-owner.ts holds that reading as a pure function with a unit test\nover an event literal, and ../sentry.ts wires it in.\n\nA remote gets its own project by setting `SENTRY_DSN_<NAME>` - in .env for a\nlocal build, as a secret for CI. Unset, its build reports into the shell''s\nproject like before, so nothing has to exist in Sentry before a remote can\nbuild. Traces are not routed: one crosses every build and stays in the app''s\nproject.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show the line a .NET frame threw on in Sentry, by embedding the sources in the PDB the Release build uploads\n\nA browser frame in Sentry shows the code around it because the Vite plugin\nuploads source maps. A .NET frame showed a method name and nothing else: the\nAPI build handed Sentry no debug files at all. It now does the equivalent -\nin Release, `EmbedAllSources` writes the source text into the portable PDB,\nand `SentryUploadSymbols` sends that PDB through the sentry-cli the Sentry\npackage ships. Sentry extracts the sources it needs and shows the frame in\ncontext, with or without the PDB beside the deployed DLL.\n\nNot `SentryUploadSources`, the documented alternative, which silently does\nnothing here. The .NET SDK puts a Source Link map into every PDB, and\nsentry-cli treats a file that has a Source Link the same as one whose text\nis embedded: it skips it. Every file has one, so it bundles nothing, prints\n\"Resolved source code for 0 debug information files\" and succeeds. Sentry\ndoes not follow the link either (symbolic #735). Api.csproj records this so\nnobody flips the switch back.\n\nThe upload hangs off the same `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` as the browser''s maps and\nhappens only in Release, so `yarn verify`, `yarn api:test` and `dotnet run`\nnever upload. `yarn api:build` is that Release build, and the CI build job\nruns it after `yarn build`. The token has to be the organization kind\n(`sntrys_...`), which is what the org''s token page issues; .env.example says\nso. Verified against a real crash: the issue in frontend-demo-api now shows\nthe `throw` line with three lines of context on either side.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start a navigation span at the click, so the remote it fetched on the way shows inside it\n\nA navigation into /account read in Sentry as a bare API call: none of the\naccount remote''s chunks appeared in the span, while the pageload of / showed\nevery one of the home remote''s. Measured, the span started 96ms after the\nclick, and the whole of that gap was the remote being fetched. A data router\nresolves a route''s `lazy` before it commits the URL, and the URL change is\nwhat Sentry''s `wrapCreateBrowserRouter` listens for; by the time the span\nexisted, every chunk had landed, and Sentry drops a resource entry that\npredates the span it would belong to.\n\nThe router''s own `instrumentations` hook sees the click. `routerInstrumentation`\nrecords when `navigate()` was called, and `beforeStartSpan` on the tracing\nintegration hands that time to the navigation span as its `startTime`, then\nclears it. Only navigation spans - a pageload starts with the document. The\nback button goes through `popstate`, never reaches the hook, and has no\nremote to fetch, so its span loses nothing by starting at the URL change.\n\nA module variable carries the time because at the moment the router calls\nthe hook there is no span to hold it - that is the gap itself. React Router''s\n`route.instrument({ lazy })` would have been the natural place to time the\nload, and it does not fire here: the router wraps only the function form of\n`lazy`, and routes.tsx uses the object form on purpose.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split the backend into two services, so a save reads as one trace across both (ADR 0004)\n\nEverything this repository showed about tracing crossed exactly one boundary,\nthe browser to the API, and one boundary is the case that works by accident.\nThe question it could not answer was whether a request still reads as one\nthing once the backend is more than one process.\n\nIt does. server/Notifications is a second ASP.NET Core service on 3002, which\nthe account API calls over HTTP when an account changes. Measured against a\nreal save, Sentry received seven spans under one trace id across two projects:\n`PUT /api/account` at 140ms in frontend-demo-api, its `db.query` spans, an\n`http.client` span of 87ms to 3002, and in frontend-demo-notifications the\n`POST /notifications/account-changed` transaction with a `notification.send`\nspan inside it.\n\nThree things do that work and none of them propagates anything by hand.\nSentry.AspNetCore continues the trace named by the `sentry-trace` and `baggage`\nheaders on an incoming request. `AddHttpClient<NotificationsClient>` turns on\nthe HTTP client factory, which Sentry hangs a handler filter on, so outgoing\nrequests carry the same headers and open an `http.client` span - a\n`new HttpClient()` would reach the service just as well and show two unrelated\ntraces. And one shared `TracesSampler` honours `IsParentSampled` before\nanything else, so a trace the browser chose to keep is kept at every hop\nrather than recorded at one and dropped at the next.\n\nThe two services share no domain type. `AccountChanged` is declared on both\nsides and the projects do not reference each other, because a shared record is\na shared assembly and a shared assembly is two services that deploy together.\nserver/Observability is the one project both reference, and it earns that by\ncarrying the Sentry setup and nothing that knows what an account is.\n\nA notification cannot fail a save: the account is already written by the time\nthe call runs, so NotificationsClient swallows every exception and logs a\nwarning. Verified with the service down - the PUT still returns 200, and the\nfailed `http.client` span inside a successful transaction is what keeps the\nfault visible. The call is awaited rather than queued, which is what keeps\nthat span inside the transaction and lets e2e/account.spec.ts read the\nnotification back out of the other process the moment the save returns.\n\nThe frontend is unchanged: no new origin, no proxy entry, no CORS.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Rename server/Api to server/Accounts, so both services are named after their subject\n\n`src/` groups by subject and AGENTS.md says so; `server/` did not, and with\ntwo services the inconsistency showed. `Notifications` named a subject, `Api`\nnamed a layer, and \"the API\" stopped being a distinguishing word the moment\nthe notifications service was one too - the comments in that folder already\nsaid \"the account API\" throughout and never \"the Api\".\n\nPlural, because the folder holds a record called `Account`. With the root\nnamespace renamed, `Account.Account` is legal C# and unpleasant, while\n`Accounts.Account` reads fine and matches the `accounts` table and the\n`AccountsDb` context already there. The EF migrations move with it: their\nnamespace is what `dotnet ef` would generate next, so the committed files and\nthe next generated one agree.\n\nThe scripts moved with the folder, or renaming it would only have moved the\ninconsistency. `accounts:start` addresses the service; `server:test` and\n`server:build` address the solution, which is what they always did - both run\nover server.slnx and have covered the notifications service since it existed.\n\nThree names deliberately left alone. The Sentry project stays\n`frontend-demo-api`: renaming a project breaks saved queries and alerts for a\ncosmetic gain, and the DSN is unchanged either way. ADR 0002 still says\n`server/Api/`, which was true the day it was written, and ADR 0004 now records\nwhere it went. docs/intentional-tests/ are dated run logs that already carry\nthe older `server/api.ts` from before the .NET move; they are records of a run,\nnot documentation of a layout.\n\n`yarn verify`, `yarn test`, `yarn server:test` and `yarn e2e` all green after\nthe move.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Rewrite the notifications service in Node, so one trace crosses two runtimes (ADR 0005)\n\nSame port, same three routes, same wire contract - `node:http` and TypeScript\nrun through Node''s type stripping, instrumented by @sentry/node. The account API\nis untouched, NotificationsClient.cs included, which is the cheapest possible\ndemonstration that ADR 0004 drew the boundary in the right place.\n\nADR 0004 split the backend to ask whether the observability story survives more\nthan one service, but both services were ASP.NET Core - the case most likely to\nwork by accident. A trace is linked by its id: not by its project, which ADR\n0004 showed, and now not by its runtime either.\n\nserver/Observability/ goes with it. It existed to hold the Sentry setup both\nservices shared; with one .NET service left, a library with one consumer is not\na boundary, so SentrySetup.cs moves in beside Program.cs.\n\n* Pass the commit sha as SENTRY_RELEASE, so every part names one build\n\nA release is global to the organisation and carries events from every project\nunder it, so one sha is what lets a deploy be compared across the five. The Vite\nplugin would derive the same value from git on its own; naming it in the build\njob is what makes the three frontend builds and the two backends agree on a\nstring rather than each deriving one.\n\nThat reaches the browser only - Vite stamps it into each bundle at build time.\nThe two backend services read it from their own environment at startup, and they\ndo not degrade the same way without it: the account API falls back to\n`Accounts@1.0.0+<sha>` from its assembly version, the Node service reports no\nrelease at all. .env.example says so, and says why a deployment should carry the\nsha into each artifact rather than set it from the platform''s own config.\n\nIt also records what the move to Node did to source context: that service needs\nno auth token and no maps, because it runs the TypeScript it shipped and\n@sentry/node reads the file off the disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Move the design tokens into packages/tokens, so the workspace split has a leaf to start from\n\nFirst step of the yarn workspaces + Turborepo migration. The tokens belong to\nno vertical and every build has to compile the same file, which makes them the\nsmallest package that proves the split works.\n\nThe package exports `./tokens.stylex`, not `.`, and the imports read\n`@demo/tokens/tokens.stylex`. That is not decoration: the StyleX babel plugin\ntests the IMPORT SPECIFIER for the `.stylex` suffix before it resolves\nanything, so `@demo/tokens` is rejected outright - \"Could not resolve the path\nto the imported file\" - even though Node resolves the package fine. StyleX''s\nown `aliases` option cannot help, because the suffix check runs first.\n\nThe generated CSS variable names change once, because the babel plugin hashes\nthe file path relative to `rootDir` and the file moved. What matters is that\nthe three builds still agree: shell, account and home each emit the same 26\nvariables, which is the invariant `unstable_moduleResolution` exists to\nprotect. `stylex.config.ts` stays at the repository root so all three keep one\n`rootDir`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Take the route tree and the handlers as arguments, so the test harness stops depending on the app\n\nsrc/testing/ is about to become a package, and it could not: it imported\n../routes, ../query-client and @account/mocks/handlers, while the tests of\naccount, home AND layout all import it back. One source tree hides that; two\npackages make it a cycle, and Turborepo rejects a cyclic task graph.\n\nThe harness now takes what it mounts:\n\n- `renderRoute(path, app)` and `renderComponent(ui, app)` receive an\n  `AppUnderTest` - the route factory and the QueryClient factory - instead of\n  importing them. src/app-under-test.ts is the one place the shell names its\n  own pair, and a vertical''s test passes it.\n- `createWorker(handlers)` replaces the module-level `setupWorker(...handlers)`.\n  vitest.setup.ts creates it, being outside every package and therefore the\n  place the harness and the account''s mocks can meet. A test that overrides a\n  response now reads `worker().use(...)`; the handlers still go to\n  `setupWorker`, so `resetHandlers()` returns to the same list as before.\n\nsrc/testing/ now imports react, react-router, react-query, msw and\nvitest-browser-react, and nothing of this app''s.\n\nThe edge this makes visible was always real: a vertical''s integration test\nmounts the SHELL''s route tree, deliberately, so a renamed path fails in the\ntest rather than in production. It is now written at the call site.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add Turborepo over the scripts that exist, so the three builds stop running twice\n\nNo file moves: this wires turbo to today''s root scripts and proves the cache\nbefore anything is split.\n\nThe measured result, on this tree: `build` goes from 17.6s to 5ms on a second\nrun and restores dist/ from the cache, which is the duplication ci.yml pays\nfor today - the `build` job and the `e2e` job each run the same three Vite\nbuilds. A real edit to a source file misses, and reverting it hits again.\n\n`.turbo/` had to be gitignored for any of that to work. A root task''s default\ninputs are the whole root package, so the cache directory was hashing itself:\nevery run wrote into it, which changed the hash, which guaranteed the next run\nmissed. The two .NET tasks get explicit `inputs` for the same reason - without\nthem any frontend edit would rebuild the server.\n\n`devEngines.packageManager` is required by turbo to resolve the workspace. It\nalso makes npm refuse to run in this repo, which is correct and costs nothing:\nnothing here invokes npx.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Extract packages/testing, so the harness is a package every vertical can depend on\n\nThe third namespace of alias.ts is gone: `@demo/testing/render-route` is a real\npackage name that yarn''s workspace symlink resolves, so neither alias.ts nor\ntsconfig.json `paths` has to spell it out. Two namespaces left, and this file\ngoes with the last one.\n\npackages/query-client, which the plan called for, turns out not to be needed.\nInjecting the QueryClient factory through AppUnderTest already removed the\nharness''s reason to reach for it, and the shell is now its only consumer - so\nit stays where it is. One package fewer than planned.\n\nThe harness is declared in the ROOT devDependencies rather than in each\nvertical''s. That is deliberate and not laziness: layout''s tests import the\naccount''s mocks while the account''s tests mount the shell''s routes, so any\napp-to-app test dependency would be a cycle, and turbo traverses\ndevDependencies. `test` is a root task with no topology, and the root package\nis outside the apps'' graph, so this is where the test tier composes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Split src/ into apps/shell, apps/account and apps/home, and delete alias.ts\n\nThe three verticals move together rather than one per commit, because the test\ntier entangles them: the layout''s tests use the account''s mocks while the\naccount''s tests mount the shell''s routes. Split one at a time, each commit\nwould have carried a `../../../../src/...` import waiting for the next one.\n\nalias.ts is gone, and with it the `paths` block in tsconfig.json. The\nduplication the file''s own header called \"the one thing to keep in sync\" is\nnot replaced by anything: `@demo/account/mocks/db-utils` is a package name,\nwhich yarn resolves through node_modules and TypeScript reads out of that\npackage''s `exports`. Playwright, which had no alias config and read the\nmapping straight from tsconfig.json, now resolves the way everything else\ndoes. What each package exposes is its `exports` field - a real surface, where\nbefore every file of every vertical was reachable from everywhere.\n\n`remotesFromSource` survives, moved into vitest.config.ts, which was always\nits only consumer. It is also why the packages are SCOPED: a package literally\nnamed `account` would make `import(\"account/pages\")` resolve as a real\nsubpath in the shell''s build and silently bundle what must be federated - the\nsame failure alias.ts guarded against, with nothing left to remove.\n\nTwo things had to change for the builds to survive their new cwd:\n\n- StyleX''s postcss half globs against `process.cwd()` unless the pattern is\n  absolute, and each app now builds with its own directory as cwd. A relative\n  `packages/tokens/**` would have matched nothing and emitted a stylesheet\n  with no rules - no error, just an unstyled page. `fromRoot()` in\n  stylex.config.ts anchors every pattern at the repository root.\n- Each app writes into its OWN dist/. Turbo restores a cache entry by\n  overwriting the task''s declared outputs, so three builds sharing one\n  directory would have had one restore clobber another''s artefacts.\n\nMeasured after the split: 66/73/33 CSS rules per build and the same 26 token\nvariables in all three, identical to before it; three builds in parallel\ninstead of three in series; and `turbo run build` twice is FULL TURBO. The e2e\nsuite passes, which is the tier that proves home still fetches the account''s\npreview over the wire rather than bundling it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Cache turbo in CI and write down what the split decided (ADR 0006)\n\nThe `build` and `e2e` jobs carry .turbo between runs, keyed per job. Per job\non purpose: `build` runs with the Sentry secrets and `e2e` without them, those\nvariables are in the task''s `env`, so the two hash differently and must not\nshare an entry - a token-bearing build served from a token-less one would\nupload nothing and say nothing. The win is a commit that touches only server/,\ndocs/ or evals/, where both jobs restore the three builds instead of running\nthem.\n\nADR 0006 records the decision and the four things about it that are invisible\nin the code: why the packages must stay scoped, why the tokens are imported as\n`@demo/tokens/tokens.stylex` and no other spelling, why StyleX''s postcss globs\nare absolute, and why the test tier composes at the root rather than between\napps. It also corrects what the first commit of this series claimed: the\nStyleX plugin hashes a `defineVars` file by the nearest package.json above it,\nnot by `rootDir`, which is only a fallback when there is no package.json at\nall. Same observed effect, different mechanism - and it means the tokens are\nnow stable by construction rather than by a config value.\n\nAGENTS.md''s Verticals section is rewritten for packages; the Tests section is\nuntouched, because the contract it states - the FILENAME, not the folder,\ndecides a test''s tier - survived the migration intact.\n\nserver/Notifications/ stays where it is rather than becoming a workspace. It\nis a Node process with no build, its sibling is .NET and cannot move, and\ndocs/adr/0005 put the two together because they are one subject seen from two\nruntimes. Moving one for symmetry with apps/ would split that pair for nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Declare the test-tier dependencies off the root package, so an edit stops invalidating every build\n\nListing the apps in the ROOT devDependencies - which the previous commit did\nto avoid an app-to-app cycle - silently cost the cache the whole migration was\nfor. An internal dependency of the root package feeds turbo''s GLOBAL hash, so\n`hashOfInternalDependencies` moved on any workspace change and every task in\nthe repository missed. Editing one line of apps/account rebuilt all three\napps.\n\nMeasured before and after, on the same one-line edit to AccountPage.tsx:\n\n  before   @demo/account  MISS   @demo/shell  MISS   @demo/home  MISS\n  after    @demo/account  MISS   @demo/shell  HIT    @demo/home  HIT\n\n`@demo/testing` moves to each app''s own devDependencies, where it belongs: the\nharness is a leaf, so that edge is acyclic and scoped to the app that uses it.\nThe app-to-app edges - the layout''s tests reaching for the account''s mocks,\nthe account''s tests mounting the shell''s routes - stay undeclared. Yarn\nsymlinks every workspace member into the root node_modules whether or not\nanything declares it, so they resolve regardless; ADR 0006 now records that as\na known gap rather than leaving it to be rediscovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add apps/mobile as one Expo app, over a packages/account-core shared with the web\n\nThe account''s platform-free half - the Zod schema, the French-phone parser,\nthe API client, the useAccount query and the MSW handlers - moves out of\napps/account into packages/account-core, a leaf like packages/testing and\npackages/tokens. Both apps/account and apps/mobile import it; nothing else is\nshared. The one piece that had a platform in it, `window.location.origin` as\nthe API origin, becomes `setApiBaseUrl`, called by the host that has no window.\n\napps/mobile is a single Expo app (Expo Router, Unistyles, @sentry/react-native)\nrather than three remotes, because a binary has no independent deployment for\nModule Federation to buy. ADR 0007 records that, and what is deliberately\nwritten twice: the tokens (packages/tokens/tokens.native.ts beside the StyleX\nvars), the Sentry setup and the QueryClient.\n\nWiring around it:\n\n- `yarn test:native` runs the jest-expo tier against the same MSW handlers,\n  and CI runs it beside `yarn server:test`.\n- `yarn verify` type-checks apps/mobile, which the root tsconfig excludes.\n- turbo''s build forwards EXPO_PUBLIC_*, since Expo inlines them into the\n  bundle; the mobile dev script is `start`, not `dev`, so the web fan-out does\n  not bring Metro up.\n- @babel/core goes back to 7, which is what the Expo toolchain runs on.\n- The web Sentry projects are renamed demo-web-<remote>-frontend, to sit\n  beside the mobile app''s own project.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Start the API without a DSN, and give the first native test time to load the app\n\nBoth failures only show up where nothing is configured, which is why they\npassed locally and failed on every CI run.\n\nThe account API threw at startup when SENTRY_DSN was unset: \"You must supply\na DSN to use Sentry\". SentrySetup.cs said Sentry was off unless the variable\nwas set, but this SDK treats a null DSN as a mistake, not as off. Locally .env\nalways sets one. In CI nothing does, so the e2e job''s web server never came\nup. An empty string is how the SDK is told to be off.\n\nThe first test in each native file timed out at jest''s default 5s. It is the\none that pays for loading expo-router, the screens and the mock network,\nwhich takes 2.6s on a laptop and more than 5s on a CI runner running three\nfiles at once. The rest of each file passed in milliseconds.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtjpFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQkgQAFUJREQt5ydYSi0KLT/z0BbT\nWueszcDHIQiuzC6/G2gimkjw1l9xDfIQnmJXBo+fZUq0zW1twChCh1qZiVwo/TqW\n1UVGEecnf/wegA9rHxc31azWm2JYTtNf4tsPzROlFRRF5zJFbGtWlPJcqmAVlACC\nskcdSZUoQEwNWgiG/xHKOCu8rGdxyp1Ezs63uVysvGpDo20dQ6MssPQpzna3x4Tg\nPHuRGF+hv+r6uS1Uqpr8n/1FPOMJC/CKAUdDSR6hQMGWDi13IEQzb7kHWuBvuoSJ\n8t3M5ooNcZ3ox8VwZWNgqJjVY6+lH3+owUXy0V9cE2/b+Lez76LajHiHVZ5CWqNB\nGmhUl2L2SQz7/PO/CCCa92T7USaWk2yJtI4KtlH/mjYbJX1ewj7iY12q7xT/4nbF\n4WGQ6Lgr030KlObJFYg4V/Xs0QL6a3TaDDHd931ltGlwC7EYx93p1yx2ijvt+bPN\nW2yrWB2SiPwEXRTx1aA1mxX0lv2RrHWQumEWIPqdODzMjAQbjxDynpZWvfhogtmK\nlrvFPMZiki+t5qd/8C9asJ6QnvcZBSnHxO8KVaabtnwUiOoZNMMR1EigHe4YXy5y\nQ+x6se0tgC9T+ABbYteCelDVzEI89hRymnp7WX/9bmqsTlvtBlwT/lb0XDT7vqVa\nf7i6rYBxLrswOJFTzXun\n=ldQ1\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T09:09:26Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKDZlMzViNzc1N2M0MDcwZTdjNDY3ZGVhODFiMTM0OGI1ZDlmZTJjZGM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4","sha":"d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4"}],"sha":"6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/6e35b7757c4070e7c467dea81b1348b5d9fe2cdc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:02:03Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:02:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Route through React Router, and say out loud what that costs (#15)\n\nSwaps TanStack Router for react-router 8 in data mode: plain route objects\nhanded to createBrowserRouter, a pathless layout route for the shell, and\ncreateMemoryRouter where the browser tests used to build a memory history.\nRouterProvider comes from react-router/dom rather than react-router, because\nthat one wires in react-dom''s flushSync.\n\nThe nav moves from Link with activeProps/inactiveProps to NavLink, whose\n`className` and `style` are separate callbacks - so the active/inactive pair of\nstylex.props calls is picked once and read twice. `activeOptions={{ exact }}`\nbecomes `end`, and the router still writes the aria-current=\"page\" the\nNavigation test asserts.\n\nWhat is lost is worth naming rather than discovering later: the\n`declare module ... interface Register` that made `<Link to=\"...\">` a compile\nerror on a typo has no equivalent in data mode - React Router types links in\nframework mode only, which would mean a Vite plugin and a generated types\ndirectory. The Navigation integration test and the e2e specs are the filet\nnow, and routes.tsx says so.\n\nOne e2e assertion had to move. React Router pushes the URL before React\nrepaints, so the first field assertion after the edit-link click could still be\nlooking at the summary''s <dd aria-label=\"Name\"> - and toHaveValue fails\noutright on \"Not an input element\" instead of retrying its way to the real\ninput. The fix is the arrival assertion that step was missing, the one the\n/account step already had.\n\nyarn verify, yarn test (71) and yarn e2e (3) are green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"da7fd19e6581db5530ba54e1492bf439634247f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/da7fd19e6581db5530ba54e1492bf439634247f5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4","verification":{"payload":"tree da7fd19e6581db5530ba54e1492bf439634247f5\nparent 922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790193723 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790193723 +0200\n\nRoute through React Router, and say out loud what that costs (#15)\n\nSwaps TanStack Router for react-router 8 in data mode: plain route objects\nhanded to createBrowserRouter, a pathless layout route for the shell, and\ncreateMemoryRouter where the browser tests used to build a memory history.\nRouterProvider comes from react-router/dom rather than react-router, because\nthat one wires in react-dom''s flushSync.\n\nThe nav moves from Link with activeProps/inactiveProps to NavLink, whose\n`className` and `style` are separate callbacks - so the active/inactive pair of\nstylex.props calls is picked once and read twice. `activeOptions={{ exact }}`\nbecomes `end`, and the router still writes the aria-current=\"page\" the\nNavigation test asserts.\n\nWhat is lost is worth naming rather than discovering later: the\n`declare module ... interface Register` that made `<Link to=\"...\">` a compile\nerror on a typo has no equivalent in data mode - React Router types links in\nframework mode only, which would mean a Vite plugin and a generated types\ndirectory. The Navigation integration test and the e2e specs are the filet\nnow, and routes.tsx says so.\n\nOne e2e assertion had to move. React Router pushes the URL before React\nrepaints, so the first field assertion after the edit-link click could still be\nlooking at the summary''s <dd aria-label=\"Name\"> - and toHaveValue fails\noutright on \"Not an input element\" instead of retrying its way to the real\ninput. The fix is the arrival assertion that step was missing, the one the\n/account step already had.\n\nyarn verify, yarn test (71) and yarn e2e (3) are green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDA7CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATaAQAKkSOHEnY/Lv2B5alsfuDU5e\nPXzrlOZRQGQBsREyqIv9AUZmNic93PmVm8dpxt7A4He7xdMciu6HQhzfMk8bG3Gs\ncK3v1rjlpaIJZ33MCf9Ug5H0SijuYww+sJDdL27pKBZFhekOAGW7eHytrQITbAC3\nZjimOBQdPPK1R0G2CbxrwZ3X4cE3uvqngpABLPgGtuqdsSXEObgmfvgL/JvBafjH\n8sHCfrFIlbHhw4wQSlHEh37lHKbxOKWTFs3inmKGBf9HeqdbZorut6Rw1HZOsBJU\nM3vhEULeEr6tSJZKN2Wiel2s3wvEUEuiHQPWGg2OEQB7qXHO8yIMgC9SiMlCwEXP\n9O6PnbPFpzCvvttjwDVOSAoEVbKQvU7K6jBVyci7pKdt1SXT4ywTs96Cza5AdemH\noHEPwgEszdibZy/IIBiibBvyPlorkl9k59sydeu9oD51aCk6ScuNBApuZMGCRxE4\nkxSomlFSd5EueR1uuCcn7HkxIT264Up6j+CkBoPNGUSg+3jcbfdYoF4DPbgrj0W8\nyrWOFAmx0WZqnW1sEOOb/TDZnzDVwoF9XO7Uh7EWlpYUDbrQNGbud4cmEu93ZKRd\nrymg98FNsG0skg4mTxsZOKhoWpYk+ptoSbPKpXrvzgyU4GNPpmIR9jyLW0/cyNE9\niYaSXL6ominWEVJluYGs\n=bCEh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T20:02:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKGQzMWVkNDM3MDcwOGMyYTQzYWY0ODQxY2I1ZmI4NDQ5NmUzMDdjZjQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470","sha":"922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470"}],"sha":"d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/d31ed4370708c2a43af4841cb5fb84496e307cf4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T19:19:35Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T19:19:35Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Store the API''s accounts in Postgres through Drizzle (ADR 0001) (#14)\n\n* Store the API''s accounts in Postgres through Drizzle (ADR 0001)\n\nserver/api.ts kept its accounts in a Map and forgot them on restart. It now\nkeeps them in PostgreSQL, reached through Drizzle over pg, with migrations\ngenerated by drizzle-kit into server/migrations/ and applied by the API when\nit starts. The per-session key that isolates parallel E2E specs stays, as\nthe primary key column.\n\nserver/ stays flat: schema.ts (the table), store.ts (the queries, taking the\ndatabase as an argument), db.ts (connection and startup migration). The\nstore is unit-tested against PGlite with the committed migrations, so the\nNode tier needs no service. Locally Postgres comes from compose.yaml via\n`yarn db:start`; in CI the e2e job declares a postgres service.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add `yarn start`: database, API and dev server in one terminal\n\nStarting the project locally now takes three commands in the right order,\nand two of them have to stay running. `yarn start` waits for Postgres, then\nruns the API and Vite side by side through concurrently, so one Ctrl+C stops\nboth and either one dying stops the other.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"71f009271aaf50f2e74a617abd9d62727a305bc9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/71f009271aaf50f2e74a617abd9d62727a305bc9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470","verification":{"payload":"tree 71f009271aaf50f2e74a617abd9d62727a305bc9\nparent d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1790191175 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790191175 +0200\n\nStore the API''s accounts in Postgres through Drizzle (ADR 0001) (#14)\n\n* Store the API''s accounts in Postgres through Drizzle (ADR 0001)\n\nserver/api.ts kept its accounts in a Map and forgot them on restart. It now\nkeeps them in PostgreSQL, reached through Drizzle over pg, with migrations\ngenerated by drizzle-kit into server/migrations/ and applied by the API when\nit starts. The per-session key that isolates parallel E2E specs stays, as\nthe primary key column.\n\nserver/ stays flat: schema.ts (the table), store.ts (the queries, taking the\ndatabase as an argument), db.ts (connection and startup migration). The\nstore is unit-tested against PGlite with the committed migrations, so the\nNode tier needs no service. Locally Postgres comes from compose.yaml via\n`yarn db:start`; in CI the e2e job declares a postgres service.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Add `yarn start`: database, API and dev server in one terminal\n\nStarting the project locally now takes three commands in the right order,\nand two of them have to stay running. `yarn start` waits for Postgres, then\nruns the API and Vite side by side through concurrently, so one Ctrl+C stops\nboth and either one dying stops the other.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtCZICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8IYQAH9CH4953rdIs2nl2yl3cPbq\nHnydvUOBAf7EWd9HieV2NhMD/K/0r1wRlJCHJf4BfspzZqImA0g64wYDI75H4CYc\nLwIUPP7u1KCk6r6Q3iEeBWEW620KExZafFVLqUJEH7tPMbHQcFqy3ZySQ1c2ADfa\ngWUJT6VRZGBWf48Ty0EVNqy1hst+1k3T+j2wDbsTDeKOOztEvv0bH07jDOvtjxSG\ncaLiWKyvJ7PnmVUIHixdj7vusUK/oHZtZ/3ggKywrlRPUlHkFhCZ5Ld7Q81TPh3O\n4ME2vl4+a52b9GLGPoOOmsw