.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/922ff272f58798f0b36bce35604de40801d69470"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T15:00:17Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T15:00:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Ask evals.config.ts what it says, not that it moved, before paying for a run (#12)\n\n* Ask evals.config.ts what it says, not that it moved, before paying for a run\n\nThe `changes` job widened to every gate in both arms — ten paid jobs — whenever\n`evals.config.ts` appeared in a diff, on the reasoning that the file decides\nwhat a trial is. Most of it does. `onlineJudge` does not: `online.ts` is its\nonly reader, and no benchmark gate can see it. Flipping that one key bought ten\ntrials to re-measure a constant.\n\nA filename cannot tell the two apart, so the job stops asking it one.\n`fingerprintOf` hashes the config keys a trial can read, `yarn evals\n--trial-fingerprint` prints this repository''s, and the workflow compares the\nhash at the merge base with the hash at HEAD. Equal hashes buy nothing; both\nland in the job summary, so a run nobody expected can be read back.\n\nThe excluded keys are a denylist, not an allowlist, and the direction is the\nsafety. A key added later and forgotten is hashed, which costs a run nobody\nneeded — once, visibly. An allowlist that forgot one would skip the run that\nwould have caught a regression and report the stale pass rate as current.\n`config.unit.test.ts` holds that invariant, including a key it has never heard\nof.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Change onlineJudge config\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"409e744e7a4d5598919999d361a7da34a8f077b4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/409e744e7a4d5598919999d361a7da34a8f077b4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9","verification":{"payload":"tree 409e744e7a4d5598919999d361a7da34a8f077b4\nparent ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1789743617 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789743617 +0200\n\nAsk evals.config.ts what it says, not that it moved, before paying for a run (#12)\n\n* Ask evals.config.ts what it says, not that it moved, before paying for a run\n\nThe `changes` job widened to every gate in both arms — ten paid jobs — whenever\n`evals.config.ts` appeared in a diff, on the reasoning that the file decides\nwhat a trial is. Most of it does. `onlineJudge` does not: `online.ts` is its\nonly reader, and no benchmark gate can see it. Flipping that one key bought ten\ntrials to re-measure a constant.\n\nA filename cannot tell the two apart, so the job stops asking it one.\n`fingerprintOf` hashes the config keys a trial can read, `yarn evals\n--trial-fingerprint` prints this repository''s, and the workflow compares the\nhash at the merge base with the hash at HEAD. Equal hashes buy nothing; both\nland in the job summary, so a run nobody expected can be read back.\n\nThe excluded keys are a denylist, not an allowlist, and the direction is the\nsafety. A key added later and forgotten is hashed, which costs a run nobody\nneeded — once, visibly. An allowlist that forgot one would skip the run that\nwould have caught a regression and report the stale pass rate as current.\n`config.unit.test.ts` holds that invariant, including a key it has never heard\nof.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Change onlineJudge config\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrVIBCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA//0QAJpp/OeI51yAcRixZ8mwDJYf\nWwY79iNigEfxjo5avXKZXoN/tg5N3OLLsooKaQukWrfWZJPpCyHjKzWBRqZv8U+K\nlmlwKxVvAtdJ2EFkUkCeNspcZ/52Ov8C/oF4wdvSnQADOpmCGEnWPjqoxhOD4lIH\nj9DhhZXqHjIwVNTMTkSCD795HZBE35oJZZLSYnoVWvxCtViS9Rf/Ia76DxcKoqkF\n8/jCeeWbwcziTFA31fJP0d+Chojcrc9Del8yaldTjeoeKgUDeJOuDZVzWfQpHk26\nMOymCj6+hBXqV7peDlnurEoI93Faoa/lSn3bj2hWgS0r/dKEeKHmfocOX+MkqCQ9\nSg8rCrM2uP2TwA0dGslyPomSa0r+Ngzhkh9BjpJm0UlyMqX213pLM/C0BFvzxDIs\nOHI+cXlQjotL4qrhSry8Cq843FmJf0mnLpEAFZxY8q+4QOhubEfLOEQrsGrSdOhQ\nvq/7hPSPe9NVdTH4X7FjCMzCXiWccal8cmmHInu1+sMPTc8K9x13fHjaYOLJosWQ\nqCEMDhuG63iPT83ynqv5/XjoKQy3f1niRU63P/bbK0qC9BXKmCvjh73XUFJip1W6\noYzft/8kpKLt7hsDYieuAMMP3DL7lkMAE8AmclVDFpwV9NMKDVhl9KPASl5SwX2V\n8/drbHlcWsA8JBxrgZpr\n=avF+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T15:00:18Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKGQxZjYwNDZiOGEzMzI2NjA5MzU4Nzc3NDI1ZGQ0ZWYyYjM2MGNkZjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721","sha":"ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721"}],"sha":"d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/d1f6046b8a3326609358777425dd4ef2b360cdf9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T14:37:45Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T14:37:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Measure whether the skills land, with an eval suite and a CI gate (#10)\n\n* Measure whether the skills land, with an eval suite and a CI gate\n\n`yarn verify` answers \"are the rules followed?\" for the rules a linter covers.\nNothing answered it for the ~75 that no linter can reach. `evals/` does: a task\nis a prompt that tempts an agent to break one rule, run through `claude -p` in a\nworktree from HEAD, graded on the diff it produced.\n\nThree files, three jobs. `tasks.ts` holds the prompts, what each expects, and the\ngrader. `trial.ts` owns isolation and the agent run. `run.ts` owns Langfuse — one\nsuite run is a dataset run, so the pass rate is a line over time rather than a\nnumber that scrolls away.\n\nTwo things make a score readable a month later. `--arm without-skills` strips the\nrules from the worktree, which is the only way to tell a skill that caused a pass\nfrom a model that already knew the rule. And every run records the conditions it\nwas produced under: the model is pinned, the CLI version, the runner, and the\nhost of `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` are stored beside the score, because a CLI upgrade\nthat moves one arm reads as a skill regression otherwise.\n\nThe workflow runs the three gates as a matrix on PRs that touch the rules\nthemselves, and stays informative: `run.ts` exits zero whatever the score, so a\nred gate never blocks a merge while n=1 makes it noisy. A failing job there is a\nbroken harness, not a regressed skill.\n\nEach rule file''s `impact:` is what a gate is worth, so four testing rules are\nre-tiered down to what they actually cost when broken.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stop paying for the same dependencies twelve times a pull request\n\nA pull request installed everything twelve times: once in each of the six jobs,\nand once more inside each of the six eval worktrees. The measured shape was\nabsurd — `verify` spent 20s installing to do 2s of work, `test` spent 61s to do\n10s.\n\nTwo fixes, one per layer.\n\nChromium is now cached. It was ~150 MB fetched fresh in both jobs that need it,\n41s and 21s against 10s and 9s of actual testing. The key is the lockfile hash,\nwhich is coarser than the Playwright version alone — an unrelated bump\nre-downloads the browser — but it is the only key that cannot go stale against\nthe installed `@playwright/test`. `browsers:install` still runs on a hit: it is\nidempotent, and `--with-deps` pulls OS packages a cache cannot hold.\n\nA trial''s worktree now symlinks the dependencies already installed beside it. It\nis a checkout of HEAD on the same machine, so its `node_modules` was identical to\nthe one the suite was running from, rebuilt from scratch for every trial — in CI,\nsix times in series inside billed job time. `yarn verify` and `yarn test` resolve\nthrough the link unchanged, nothing in a trial writes into `node_modules`, and\n`git worktree remove` unlinks it without touching the target. Proven by running\nboth suites inside a symlinked worktree: verify green, 7 files and 45 tests\ngreen, and the shared tree intact afterwards.\n\nThe fallback install kept for a suite running without its own dependencies now\nsays `--frozen-lockfile`. It said `--immutable`, which is Yarn Berry''s spelling:\nYarn 1.22 ignores it in silence, so the one install that had to be pinned to the\nlockfile was the only one that wasn''t.\n\nNeither job is serialised behind a shared setup job, which is what a \"build once\"\nanswer would have cost. There is no build to share here — `verify` is `tsc\n--noEmit`, `test` transforms its own sources, `e2e` builds its preview through\nPlaywright''s `webServer` — and shipping `node_modules` between jobs as an\nartifact is slower than installing it warm.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Name a gate after the skill it gates, and run only the gates a change touched\n\nA gate was named after a rule family — `skill-effects-gate`, `skill-rerender-gate`,\n`skill-modern-js-gate` — and a rule family matches no path on disk. So the\nworkflow could only filter on `.claude/skills/**` as a whole, and editing one\nreact rule ran the javascript gate too: six trials and real money to score rules\nnobody had changed.\n\nA gate is now a skill, spelled exactly as its directory: `react` and `javascript`\ntoday, the other three declared in `GATES` and waiting for a first task.\n`.claude/skills/react/**` in a diff and the react gate are the same word, which\nis the whole point — the `changes` job intersects the skill directories in the\ndiff with `yarn evals --list-gates` and builds its matrix from the result. One\nreact rule now runs one gate. A change to `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` or the harness\nstill runs all of them, because those move any gate, and a pull request that\ntouched no gated skill runs none.\n\n`--list-gates` exists so the workflow does not restate the gate list. `tasks.ts`\nstays the one place a gate is declared, which is the failure `alias.ts` and\ntsconfig `paths` already warn about in this repo: half a declaration wired, the\nother half rotting. The one exception is the `workflow_dispatch` dropdown, which\ncannot be computed and says so.\n\nThe gate is deliberately coarser than the rule now: a `react` run averages\neffects and re-render. Nothing diagnostic is lost, because a per-task score is\nnamed after the task''s `api` — `effects_gate`, `memoization_gate` — never after\nits gate, so each rule keeps its own line in Langfuse.\n\nThe cost of the rename is the run-level `pass_rate` history: a gate is a Langfuse\ndataset, and three datasets become two new ones. That history is three runs deep\ntoday and would only get more expensive to discard.\n\n`intentional-tests/` is not a gate. It is not one of the five skills `AGENTS.md`\ndeclares, and no task gates it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Let a broken harness turn the job red\n\nThe Evals run on 9ba6a14 reported success. Both gates had died before their first\ntrial, on a Langfuse 409: dataset item ids are unique per project across\ndatasets, so renaming a gate could not move its items into the new dataset. The\njob was green anyway, and the failure was found by a human reading the logs.\n\nThe cause is not the 409. `bash -e` takes a pipeline''s status from its last\ncommand, and the gate step pipes the suite through `tee` to build the step\nsummary. `tee` always succeeds, so nothing `run.ts` does with its exit code can\nreach the runner.\n\nThis is the exact inversion of what the file claims two comments earlier: a\nfailing job here is meant to mean a broken harness — a missing secret, a CLI that\nwould not install — and it meant nothing at all.\n\n`set -o pipefail` restores it, and the informative-not-blocking property survives\nuntouched, because it was never the shell''s doing: `run.ts` prints the pass rate\nand exits zero whatever the score. A red gate stays green; a harness that cannot\nrun goes red.\n\nThe `changes` job gets the same line. It pipes through `sed`, `sort` and `jq`,\nand a broken link there would have selected no gates at all — a suite that\nsilently scores nothing, reported as a pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give a rule one source, so removing it removes it\n\n`AGENTS.md` restated the React Compiler rule verbatim from\n`.claude/skills/react/references/rerender.md`: write plain components, no\nhand-written `useMemo`, `useCallback` or `memo`.\n\nThe strip that makes the `without-skills` arm removes `.claude/skills` and the\nSkills section of this file. It does not reach a rule restated under a different\nheading, so both arms read the copy, and `rerender-memo` — the task that gates\nexactly this rule — was comparing a model that had been told the rule against a\nmodel that had been told the rule. The same copy sits inside the criterion of\n`rerender-move-state-down`, which fails an answer that reaches for memoization.\nTwo of the five react tasks were measuring nothing.\n\nNothing is lost by deleting it. `vite.config.ts` wires `reactCompilerPreset()`\nin plain sight, and `react/SKILL.md` already tells an agent to check a rule''s\n`requires:` against `package.json` and the build config. A fact about how this\nrepository is built belongs in the file that decides it, where that check finds\nit in either arm; a rule belongs in the skill, where the strip can take it away.\n\nThe Skills section now says so, so the copy does not come back. It is stripped\nalong with the rest, so the statement itself cannot leak into an arm.\n\nThe remaining sections were checked against all eight tasks. Verticals, StyleX\nand the tier-by-filename rule gate nothing, and no skill states them — they are\nthis repository''s own conventions and stay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Grade what a diff cannot see, and stop reading one trial as a rate\n\nThree changes to the harness, entangled in `run.ts` and committed together.\n\n**A judge, for rules a diff cannot separate.** `modern-set-operations` forced it:\nthe agent read the rule''s `requires: ES2025`, read this repository''s `lib` floor\nof ES2023, concluded the rule did not apply and wrote an array `filter` — which\nis the right answer. An agent with no skills writes the same `filter` out of\nignorance. Identical diff, opposite reasoning, one score, and the evidence sat\nunread in the closing summary.\n\nSo a judged task carries a `criterion` in prose and `judge.ts` hands a model the\ncriterion, the prompt, the diff and the summary. The criterion lives in\n`tasks.ts`, not in a Langfuse form: a CI gate has to read its own score before\nthe job ends, and a criterion is the definition of the score — it must be\nreviewable in a pull request and changeable in the same commit as the rule it\nmeasures. It is falsifiable, and that was checked rather than assumed: given the\nreal summary it passes and cites the ES2025-versus-lib sentence; given the same\ndiff with a bland summary it fails it.\n\nThe judge is pinned separately from the subject (`claude-sonnet-5`) and travels\nbeside `model` in the run metadata, because two models move in this system. It\nruns in a scratch directory with tools, MCP and slash commands disabled, so it\ncannot confirm a criterion the agent never satisfied — and that isolation is also\nwhat makes it affordable: 148,068 tokens and $0.59 as a plain question, about\n3,000 and $0.012 with the flags. A judge that cannot answer writes `judge_error`\nand no gate score at all, never a zero.\n\n**A `typescript` gate,** with `derive-vs-decouple`: a presentational component\nwhose props tempt `Pick<Account, …>`. Judge-graded, because `Pick`, `Omit`, a\nwhole-object prop and a standalone type all render identically.\n\n**`--repeat`, default 2.** A single trial is a coin flipped once. Its score is 1\nor 0 and says nothing about how repeatable it is: a gate reading 5/5 one week and\n4/5 the next has moved by roughly one standard deviation of a five-task run whose\ntrue pass probability is 0.8. Neither number is wrong and neither answers the\nquestion.\n\nEach task now runs `repeat` times and its score is the mean, so a task that\npasses once in two reads 0.5 instead of whichever trial happened to be graded.\n`pass_rate` is the mean of those means, so a task cannot count twice for having\nbeen sampled twice. Two is the smallest count that can disagree with itself;\nraise it for the tasks whose arms sit within a point of each other, which is\nprecisely what repetition answers, and drop it to 1 to exercise the harness.\n\nThe repetition index joins the model and the arm in the stored-trial key, for the\nsame reason they are there: without it the second trial overwrites the first and\n`--reuse` grades one sample as if it were several. `--reuse` counts a task as\nstored only when every repetition the run asks for is on disk. `repeat` travels\nin the run metadata — a pass rate read without knowing how many samples it\naverages says nothing about how much of its movement is noise.\n\n`trial_cost_usd` now sums a task''s repetitions, and `judge_error` carries how\nmany of them the judge swallowed, so a mean over fewer samples than the run asked\nfor says so. `TrialOutcome` loses its `skipped` variant: `runTrial` never\nproduced one, `run.ts` did, and skipping is a property of a dataset item rather\nthan of a trial.\n\nThe workflow gains a `repeat` input and 30 more minutes: the largest gate is now\nten trials rather than five. A gate costs double what it did.\n\n`docs/lint-coverage.md` maps every rule to what can check it — lint, diff grader,\nor judge — and is what the remaining tasks are written from. It stays untracked,\nand `.gitignore` says why: a trial''s worktree is a checkout of HEAD, so a tracked\ncopy would put the answer key in front of the agent being measured. In both arms\nat once, which is not a difference between them but a floor raised under both.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run a gate three tasks abreast, and stop scoring a rate limit as a regression\n\nThe react gate is five tasks run one after another, twice each, at one to\nfour minutes a trial: the wall clock of every evals run was the sum of them.\n`run.ts` takes `--max-concurrency` and CI passes three. Tasks, not trials —\na task''s repetitions stay serial, so the samples one score averages never\nrace each other for the same token. Nothing about the cost moves: the same\ntrials spend the same tokens, only closer together.\n\nCloser together is also when a shared token hits its window, and until now\na trial `claude -p` could not finish graded as a wrong answer. There is no\n`failed` any more. An agent that answered badly is `completed` with a bad\ndiff; a trial the harness could not obtain — a rate limit, the timeout, a\nCLI that would not start — is `unavailable`, writes `trial_error` and no\ngate score, the treatment `judge_error` already had. One non-zero exit is\nstill an answer: out of `--max-turns` leaves a diff, and the diff is graded.\n\nThe workflow''s matrix is now gate × arm, but a pull request only pays for\nthe arm it can move. `without-skills` strips the skill before the agent\nstarts, so a skill edit re-measures a constant there; it runs on a pull\nrequest only when the baseline itself changed — the prompts, the pinned\nmodel, the CLI pin, `AGENTS.md` — and otherwise waits for a manual run. The\nmanual inputs say `all` and `both` where they used to say nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the effort the subject and the judge think at, as the model already is\n\n`claude -p` reads `effortLevel` from the settings of whichever machine runs\nit. A laptop and a CI runner can disagree, a trial that thinks harder passes\nmore often, and the difference reads as a skill regression — the same\nfailure an unpinned model produced, on a variable nobody had named. `--effort`\nnow travels to the subject''s `claude -p`, `--judge-effort` to the judge''s,\nand both land in the run metadata beside `model` and `judgeModel`.\n\nBoth default to `high`. It is Opus 5''s own default, so the line does not\nstep. The judge stays there on purpose: it costs a cent, and a verdict that\nflips on a borderline diff costs a gate score.\n\nEffort joins the cache key. Stored trials from before carry no level in\ntheir name and are no longer served by `--reuse`: which level they ran at is\nnot known, and a guess in a filename is the false number the key exists to\nprevent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Change config eval\n\n* Put the agent under test behind one interface, and wire Codex and Copilot to it\n\nEverything below the CLI call was already agent-agnostic — the worktree, the\ndiff, the graders and the Langfuse reporting never ask who wrote the change.\nOnly three things were Claude''s: the command and the shape of its result,\nwhere the agent reads the rules from, and whether it takes a reasoning\neffort. Those are what an `Agent` now carries, and `agents.ts` is the only\nfile that knows a CLI exists. `--agent` names who is measured, and\n`--list-agents` is where CI reads the names from rather than repeating them.\n\nAn `Agent` declares what its CLI takes, and `run.ts` refuses what it does not:\n`--effort` against an agent with no efforts, `--max-turns` against one with no\nturn cap, are command-line errors rather than flags quietly dropped. Nothing\nis recorded that was not applied — the rule the model and effort pins already\nobeyed, now enforced for every agent.\n\nCodex is driven through `codex exec`, Copilot through `copilot -p`. Neither\nhas been run: both were written against the documented flags, and the pinned\nmodel ids are the ones this suite intends to measure, not ones confirmed to\nexist on any account. A wrong pin fails the trial rather than quietly\nmeasuring something else.\n\nFour things do not port, and the shape of the code says so. Where the rules\nlive: Claude pulls a skill in on demand, so the two arms are not the same\ntreatment across agents — the with/without delta travels, the absolute pass\nrate does not. Effort and turns: a flag on Claude, a config override on Codex,\nand on Copilot a settings-file key that cannot be set for one run without also\nsupplying that CLI''s model and MCP config, so Copilot declares no efforts at\nall. Cost: Claude reports dollars, Codex tokens, Copilot nothing — the flag\nthat leaves the final message alone on stdout is the one that strips the\nstats — and an agent reporting no cost publishes no `trial_cost_usd`, because\na zero reads as a free run. Saying it failed: Claude has a `subtype` and Codex\na `turn.failed` event, so both can tell a trial the harness could not obtain\nfrom an agent that answered badly; Copilot offers only an exit code, so a run\nthat fails partway and still exits zero is scored as an answer. That hole is\ndocumented where it lives and needs a structured output mode to close.\n\nCI grows an agent axis, opt-in because it multiplies against the gates and the\narms. An `evals:<agent>` label selects who runs, no label means the default\nalone, and a label naming an agent that does not exist fails the run rather\nthan quietly scoring the default. Every job still installs the Claude CLI: the\njudge stays Claude whichever agent is under test, because it is the\ninstrument, not the subject.\n\nBefore a Codex or Copilot score means anything, the rules have to reach them.\nThe Skills section of `AGENTS.md` names Claude''s Skill tool, which Codex has\nnot; Copilot does not read `.claude/skills` at all. Until that is fixed, both\narms hand them the same context and a near-zero delta is that gap rather than\na rule that stopped earning its place.\n\nAlso flips the `requires:` on the memoized-components rule to React Compiler\noff, which is what the rule is for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Ask each task in several voices, and key a trial by the prompt it was given\n\nA task carried one prompt, so a rule that survived only the phrasing it was\nwritten against scored the same as one that held up. `prompts` is a list now,\none trial per entry, and `--repeat` is the number of passes over that list\nrather than the number of trials: a task with one prompt is unchanged at two\ntrials, and a second phrasing costs two more.\n\nThe prompt index joins the cache key, beside the agent, arm, model, effort and\nrepetition already there. Without it the second variant would overwrite the\nfirst on disk, and `--reuse` would grade one sample under metadata claiming\nseveral — the false-precision failure the rest of that key exists to prevent.\nFor the same reason `cachedTaskIds` counts a task as stored only when every\nprompt and every repetition it would run is present.\n\nThe Langfuse item carries the whole list as its input, and each trial is judged\nagainst the prompt it was actually given rather than the task''s first one.\n\nThe grader read back off a dataset item now parses through a Zod schema. That\nitem was written by an earlier run, possibly under an older shape, so it is\nuntrusted input rather than a literal `tsc` has checked; the hand-rolled\nfield-by-field check it replaces stated the diff families a second time, and\nonly one of the two was ever going to be updated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Ask the repository what to measure, and the agent whether it read the rule\n\nThree changes to the harness, one seam and two things it made visible.\n\n**The seam.** `tasks.ts` held both halves of this suite: what a task *is* and\nwhat this repository''s tasks *are*. The second is content — prompts, rules,\nfamilies, what counts as an answer — and the first is machinery that knows\nnothing about React or `src/`. They are split down `evals.config.ts`, at the\nroot beside `vite.config.ts` and `vitest.config.ts`: `evals/grading.ts` says\nwhat a task looks like and how a counted answer is scored, `evals/config.ts`\nloads the config and checks it, and `tasks.ts` is repository content that sits\nin `evals/` only because 300 lines of prompt prose do not belong in a config\nfile.\n\nThe config holds two keys and the shortness is the point. Arms, agents, models,\neffort, repetition and concurrency are flags with a default in `run.ts`, and a\nkey beside a flag would be a second place a run''s conditions get decided — the\nhalf-wired failure `alias.ts` and tsconfig `paths` warn about. There is no\n`--config` either: a suite that can be pointed at two of them is a suite whose\nscores were produced under conditions nobody can read off the run.\n\n`sourcePaths` has no default, and `src/**` is wider than the file types that\nexist today. Both for one reason: a whitelist that misses a real answer scores\nit 0 rather than showing nothing, and a default that missed a repository''s\nsources would empty every diff and score every task 0 — a plausible, false\nnumber, which is the one thing this suite exists not to produce. The globs are\nprefixed `:(glob)` where they reach git, because a bare pathspec reads a double\nstar as a single one and would have graded one directory level.\n\nThe diff grader''s `added` pattern moves onto the task for the same reason the\nprompts did. The fixed list of families it replaces had four entries and two\nwere dead, left behind by tasks that had since become judged. `run.ts` is\nchecked against the config it loads only where a type cannot speak: that the\narrays hold something, and that a pattern written for a grader is one the\ngrader can compile rather than one that throws mid-run.\n\n**The strip stops asking who is reading.** `Agent` carried a `stripRules`,\non the belief that each CLI finds its rules somewhere else. All three discover\na skill the same way — by its description, pulling the body in only once they\ncommit — and what differs is which directories they scan. So the\n\"without-skills\" arm now empties `.claude/skills`, `.github/skills` and\n`.agents/skills` and takes no agent at all. Emptying a directory this\nrepository never filled costs nothing, and it means a skill later dropped into\none nobody thought to strip cannot leak into the arm that is meant to be\nwithout them.\n\nThe appended scoped-exercise line stops naming `yarn verify` and `yarn test`\nwith it. What a project''s checks are called is in its `AGENTS.md`, which the\nagent has already read.\n\n**A gate score was two hypotheses in one number.** A `*_gate` that moves is\neither the rule that stopped working or the agent that never reached for it,\nand the evidence sat unread in tool calls. The Claude adapter reads the event\nstream rather than the single object `--output-format json` returns, collects\nthe `Skill` invocations, and each trial carries the skills it loaded.\n`skill_invoked` is the mean over the trials whose agent can report — undefined\nis a CLI that cannot say, never a zero, because a zero standing in for the\ndifference reads as a skill that never fires. On `without-skills` the score\nbelongs at 0 and is the strip verifying itself: anything above it means the two\narms were never the two treatments the run claims. The stream costs no tokens;\nit decides what the CLI prints locally, not what is sent.\n\n**`--gate` is required.** It defaulted to `react`, which runs one skill out of\nfive and reports its pass rate as the suite''s. A named `--task` still settles\nthe gate on its own, and a repository with one gate has nothing to choose. A\ngate is also declared only by a task naming it now — a skill nobody wrote a\ntask for is not a gate — and a `--gate` with no skill directory behind it is a\ncommand-line error rather than a stack trace mid-run.\n\nCI adds `evals.config.ts` to both lists it appears in: the paths that trigger\nthe suite, and the ones that move a baseline and so re-run both arms.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Remove local harnais\n\n* Add eleven tasks for the unlinted CRITICAL and HIGH rules\n\ndocs/eval-candidates.md extracts, from the oxlint-config coverage doc, every\nskill rule no linter can measure and says whether its eval should count a\ncall or ask a judge. The eleven tasks are the CRITICAL and HIGH rows of it that\nhave no lint half at all and are not React Native, each with two prompt\nvoices.\n\nSeven of them are judged where the doc said grader: avoid-hasty-abstraction,\neffects-fetch-race-condition, rerender-children-as-props and the four\nperf-async rules. In each the right and wrong answers add the same lines and\ndiffer only in which function encloses a call or where an await sits. The\nthree diff graders — a literal import() specifier, an untouched shared\nhandlers file read off the diff header, and an assertion on the msw store —\nwere exercised against sample diff lines.\n\nTwo harness changes came with them. The trial now runs git add\n--intent-to-add before diffing, because git diff HEAD skips untracked files\nand a new component, locale module or test file never reached the grader.\nAnd server/ is graded as well as src/, since three javascript tasks change\nthe API process.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Put the judge behind an interface, and let the repository choose it\n\nThe judge was one pinned `claude -p` call welded into the grader. It is now an\nadapter like the agent, with `--judge-agent`, so a machine without the Claude\nCLI can run a judged gate — and so the one bias this suite could not see from\ninside can be measured: a Claude judge reading a Claude subject.\n\nA narrower interface than `Agent`, not a reuse of it. An agent is handed a\nrepository and every tool; a judge must have neither, and what buys that is\nper-CLI: the isolation flags, how a system prompt is supplied at all, and the\nshape the answer comes back in.\n\nTwo judges are wired, both probed against the installed CLI. Copilot is pinned\nto `gpt-5.4` for the reason that matters: its own default is `claude-sonnet-5`,\nso an unpinned Copilot judge would grade a Claude subject with Claude while\nappearing on the chart to be a second vendor. Codex stays absent — `codex exec`\nhas no system-prompt flag and no verified isolation to pair with one, and a\njudge adapter that is wrong moves every score in the dataset quietly, where a\nsubject adapter that is wrong fails its own trial visibly.\n\nThe same probes falsified three things the Copilot *agent* adapter claimed, and\nthose are fixed with it: `--reasoning-effort` is a flag rather than a settings\nkey, `--output-format json` is a structured mode, and `copilot skill --help`\nnames `.claude/skills` among its discovery paths — so its `with-skills` arm was\na real comparison all along. The adapter now reports which skills loaded, and a\nrun that fails partway is `unavailable` rather than a zero.\n\n`evals.config.ts` grows from three keys to seven. `defaultJudge` mirrors\n`defaultAgent`; `agents` and `judges` hold the model and the effort, keyed by\nthe CLI they belong to because a model id is a string one CLI knows and another\nhas never heard of; `maxConcurrency` is the machine''s speed rather than the\nexperiment''s shape. Every one of them is a chain — flag, then this file, then\nthe adapter''s pin — and every one lands in the run metadata, which is the test a\nkey has to pass to live there.\n\nTwo steps in the line, both deliberate. This repository now grades with Copilot\nrather than Claude, so no verdict from here is comparable to one from before.\nAnd the Copilot agent''s effort is pinned at `high` against the CLI''s own\n`medium`. `--repeat` defaults to 1: the run people actually type asks the cheap\nquestion, and the run that wants a measurement asks for the samples.\n\nCI follows `defaultJudge` rather than restating it: the Copilot CLI is installed\nin every job because the judge needs it, and each agent''s own CLI only where it\nis under test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Install the rules under test instead of inheriting them\n\nEvery eval job failed in under a second on `No skill directory at\n.claude/skills/react`, and the failure was the harness behaving correctly.\n`96327bc` took `.claude/skills/` out of this repository; the rules are a Claude\nplugin published from another one now, and a worktree cut from this one holds\nnone of them.\n\nThe silence behind that error is the real problem. The plugin installs against a\nproject path and is enabled by that project''s settings, so an agent running in\n`/tmp/eval-…` has no rules at all — and a `with-skills` arm measuring an agent\nwith no rules scores like the arm that removed them. The gate would have read\n\"this skill does nothing\", which is the plausible, false number this suite exists\nto refuse. It failed loudly instead, which is the only reason anyone noticed.\n\nSo the harness stops inheriting the rules and installs them. `skills.pin.json`\nnames the repository, the ref and the directory inside it. `skills.ts` clones it\nonce into gitignored `evals/.skills/`, resolves the ref to a commit, and fills\neach worktree''s `.claude/skills/` from that commit with `git archive`. A trial\nthen holds everything the agent read, which is what makes it a measurement\nrather than an anecdote, and the commit travels in the metadata so a score says\nwhich text produced it.\n\nFour choices in there are load-bearing, and each was measured rather than\nassumed:\n\n- **Not the clone `claude plugin` leaves in `~/.claude/plugins`.** It is shallow\n  — one commit, verified — so it can serve neither a pin other than its tip nor\n  any baseline; the CLI moves it, so it can change under a ninety-minute run;\n  and it is where a rule is authored, so it may hold uncommitted edits.\n- **`git archive <rev>:<path>`, the tree-ish form.** The two-argument form emits\n  entries under the full path, and extracting that into `.claude/skills` would\n  bury every rule three directories deep, where no agent looks and no error is\n  raised.\n- **The gates and no more, though the plugin ships eleven skills.** An extra one\n  is not neutral: `react-native` describes itself in terms of re-renders and\n  performance, which is what three of the `react` gate''s tasks are about.\n- **The rules hash keys `with-skills` and not `without-skills`.** That arm\n  installs nothing, so the revision is not one of its inputs; keying it would\n  discard the one arm worth caching every time a rule nobody ablated moved.\n\nThe pin is its own JSON file rather than a key in `evals.config.ts`, because\nbumping it is how a rule change now reaches this repository and the workflow\ntreats a config edit as a change to what a trial *is* — every gate in both arms.\nA rule edit moves one arm only. CI grows a branch for the pin, a step that clones\nthe rules with a deploy key, and loses the `sed` that derived touched gates from\na path this repository no longer has.\n\nProven on live trials rather than by inspection: the same task scores\n`skill_invoked` 1.000 with the rules installed and 0.000 in the ablated arm. That\ndelta is what was silently missing, and it is the check to run after touching any\nof this.\n\n`--arm previous-skill` leaves the CI matrix. This repository''s diff can show that\nthe pin moved but not which rule moved with it, and only the skills repository''s\nown history can say; until its pipeline drives this workflow, that arm is manual.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Clone the rules with a scoped token rather than an SSH key\n\nAzure DevOps has no per-repository deploy keys. An SSH key there belongs to an\naccount and carries everything that account can do, across the whole\norganisation, writes included — and a secret on this repository is readable by\nanyone who can edit a workflow in it. That is a credential the eval jobs do not\nneed: they read one directory of one repository.\n\nA PAT scoped to Code (Read) is the narrowest thing Azure DevOps can issue, so\nthat is what the clone step now takes, over https.\n\nThe pin keeps its SSH url, because that is what a developer''s own machine clones\nwith, and the https url is derived from it in the step rather than configured\nbeside it — the pin stays the one place the repository is named, and a\nmalformed url fails the job with a message saying so rather than cloning\nnothing.\n\nThe token reaches git through the credential helper''s file, not the url, so it\nis never on a command line, in the process table, or in a line git echoes back\nwhen a clone fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say which clone, and which ref, when the rules cannot be read\n\nA CI job failed with `unknown revision or path not in the working tree`, naming\nthe pinned ref. That message was the second of two attempts and the first was\nswallowed, so it described a ref that does exist upstream while saying nothing\nabout the clone it was looked for in.\n\nThree fixes, all of them about where an error is raised rather than what fails:\n\n- `EVALS_SKILLS_DIR` is checked to be a clone before it is used. A path that is\n  not one produced a worktree with no rules in it, which is the failure this\n  module exists to make impossible.\n- Both resolution attempts are reported. `origin/<ref>` failing and `<ref>`\n  failing say different things — an unfetched branch is not a typo — and the\n  message now carries the commands to tell them apart.\n- The clone step verifies its own result: it prints the commit count and the\n  remote branches, and fails naming the missing branch. The answer is there, not\n  three steps later.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the commit the rules were merged into, not the branch that carried them\n\nThe branch `evals/skills.pin.json` named was merged into `master` and deleted\nupstream. A local clone kept a stale remote-tracking ref, so the pin resolved\nhere and nowhere else — which is how a CI job came to report a revision that\ndoes not exist while the same command worked on a laptop.\n\nPinned to the commit rather than to `master`, and the distinction is the whole\ndesign: with a branch, a rule changes under a run with nothing in this\nrepository to show it, and the workflow branch that exists to catch a rule\nchange never fires. A commit makes a rule change arrive as an edit to this file,\nwhich is an event CI can see.\n\nNothing measured moves. The merged commit carries byte-identical rules — the\ntree hash over the five gates is `0d8318a2c77a` before and after — so the stored\ntrials keyed by it stay valid.\n\nThe clone step''s check now accepts both forms, as `skills.ts` does: a branch has\nno local head in a clone nobody checks out, and a commit has no `origin/` form.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Measure Copilot by default, and have it graded on Sonnet rather than by itself\n\nThe people these skills are written for run Copilot, so the harness now\ndefaults to it as the subject and the judge alike, and evals.config.ts stops\nrepeating a value equal to the default. The workflow_dispatch dropdown agrees.\n\nGrading gpt-5.4 with gpt-5.4 would have made the judge''s self-reading bias\ninvisible, so the Copilot judge is pinned to claude-sonnet-5: the other vendor\nthrough the CLI every CI job already installs. A claude subject under this\njudge is Claude reading Claude, and the README says to pass\n--judge-model gpt-5.4 for that run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Fail the job, and keep no run, when a verdict could not be reached\n\nThe workflow said a red job meant a broken harness, but run.ts always exited\nzero, so a Copilot token the CLI rejected left every trial unavailable and the\njob green. Now any verdict the run could not reach — a trial that would not\nrun, a judge that would not answer, a judged task cut off before its summary —\nexits non-zero. A trial that ran and failed still exits zero: that is a result.\n\nSuch a run is deleted from the Langfuse dataset rather than kept with its\ntrial_error counts, so the chart holds only runs that measured. The reasons\nstay in the report run.ts prints, which CI copies into the job summary, and\nthe traces stay with what the failed trials cost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Score every task as rule_gate, and let Langfuse mean it into the pass rate\n\nScores were named after the grader''s family, so five gates already put eleven\nmostly empty columns in the Langfuse runs list, and the fifteen families this\nrepository is heading for would have made it unreadable. Every task now writes\nthe one score rule_gate; the family goes in front of the comment and into the\nitem''s metadata, where it groups tasks without becoming a column.\n\npass_rate goes with it: with one score name, the Ø rule_gate column Langfuse\ncomputes over a run''s items is that same mean, and a second copy written by\nrun.ts said nothing more.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Revert \"Score every task as rule_gate, and let Langfuse mean it into the pass rate\"\n\nThis reverts commit f02d69b. The per-family columns are the level that will\nmatter once a family holds more than a handful of tasks, and Langfuse offers no\ngrouping by item metadata to get them back; a score named after the family is\nthe only way it shows them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Regroup the javascript and typescript families under the rules they test\n\n`sorting` and `sets` were two families for one rule, the modern-API one, and\n`types` named a gate rather than the derive-versus-decouple rule its only task\ngrades. Renamed to `modern` and `derive`, so a family reads as the rule it\nbelongs to. The scores move with them: `sorting_gate`, `sets_gate` and\n`types_gate` stop being written, and runs recorded under those names are no\nlonger comparable to what comes next.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read a verdict out of a reply that is not clean JSON, and delete the run by the name Langfuse stored\n\nThe first run to lose a verdict showed both holes. The Copilot judge on Sonnet\nanswered with a JSON object the parser rejected — a paragraph in `reason`, most\nlikely, which puts a raw newline inside a string — and the reply was recorded\n200 characters long, too short to say. The verdict is now read out of the reply\nby its `passed` boolean when the whole does not parse, and a reply that carries\nno verdict travels whole in the reason.\n\nAnd the deletion that should have followed asked for a run that did not exist:\nthe SDK stores `${name} - ${timestamp}` unless told the run name, so the run\nstayed on the chart with a 404 in the job log. The name is now passed verbatim.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Score real pull requests online, with the same graders and score names\n\n`run.ts` gives an agent an exercise and grades the answer, which says\nwhether a rule is well written. Nothing said whether the agents people\nactually use follow it on real work. `evals/online.ts` does: it takes the\nsource diff of a branch from its merge base, runs each task''s `online`\ngrader over it, and reports one Langfuse trace tagged `online` whose\nscores carry the `<family>_gate` names the offline tier writes, so the\ntwo meet in the Scores view under one name.\n\nA task''s own grader cannot read an arbitrary diff: its criterion names\nthe component the prompt pointed at, and a diff grader that expects\n`some` asks for what no pull request can be told to contain. So a task\nthat wants to be measured online carries a second grader: a `forbidden`\nsignature counted on added lines, or a `criterion` that first says when\nit applies. The judge returns `applies` before `passed`, and a \"no\" is\nno score rather than a pass. Eleven tasks carry one.\n\n`diff.ts` now owns the one definition of the diff a grader reads;\n`trial.ts` uses it too. The trace records which coding agents signed\nthe commits, from their trailers and accounts, as a filter rather than\na gate: every change is scored, whoever wrote it.\n\n`online.yml` runs it on every pull request, report-only. Whether the\njudge runs is `onlineJudge` in `evals.config.ts`, false for now: the\nonline criteria are new, and one already needed tightening after a\nsmoke test, so their verdicts are read against real diffs before they\nare trusted. `--judge on|off` wins over the key locally.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read the online judge''s reply the same tolerant way, through one reader with tests\n\nThe tolerant reading added in 64d922c went into one of two copies of the\nparser: the online judge kept the strict `JSON.parse`, so a paragraph in\n`reason` still lost the verdict when scoring a real pull request, with 200\ncharacters of the reply to explain it. Both parsers now go through one\n`readReply`, which reads `applies`, `passed` and `reason` out of the text\nwhen the whole does not parse, and keeps 2000 characters when it finds no\nverdict at all.\n\nThe reading is a pure function and now has unit tests, including the exact\nreply that broke the first run. `evals/` had no test tier, so the unit\nproject now includes it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Post each workflow''s report as one comment on the pull request, edited in place\n\nThe scores lived in the job summary and in Langfuse, neither of which a\nreviewer opens from the conversation. Each workflow now owns one comment on\nthe pull request, found again by a marker and edited on every push, so the\nreport of a superseded commit does not pile up under the review.\n\nThe online tier posts as soon as it has scored. The offline tier posts once\nevery gate has finished: each gate keeps its report as an artifact, and a\n`report` job after the matrix gathers them into one comment, rather than ten\ncomments on an `AGENTS.md` edit. Both run on `always()`, because a gate that\nbroke is the report most worth reading and its reasons are in what `run.ts`\nprinted before it exited.\n\nThe posting is a composite action both workflows use, so the lookup and the\nedit are written once.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Write the pull request comment for the developer whose pull request it is\n\nThe comment was the harness''s own report in a code block, under the names\nthe harness uses for itself. A developer reading their pull request does not\nknow an arm from a gate, and the number they wanted was three screens down.\n\nEach run now also writes what it found as data, `--report-file`, and a\nrenderer turns it into the comment: one line per rule, a verdict and one\nsentence of why, and the links to Langfuse and to the job. The offline tier\nis \"Skill benchmark\", one line per rule family with a column per arm; the\nonline tier is \"Rules check\", failures first. The names the workflows show\nsay what they measure; the names in the code still say how.\n\nA pull request with no source file in its diff no longer gets a comment\nsaying so: a first job asks `--list-files`, and the scoring job is skipped,\nwhich reads as \"not about this\" on the pull request rather than as a pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the rules check as one job, and say nothing when there is nothing to grade\n\nThe job that asked \"anything to check?\" ahead of the scoring one read badly\non the pull request, two checks where a developer expects one. The check is\none job again, as it was. A diff with no source file in it still gets no\ncomment: `online.ts` exits zero without a report in that case, and the\ncomment step reads a green job with no report as nothing to say.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the comment action its own temp file\n\nBoth workflows name their body file `comment.md` under the runner''s temp\ndirectory, and so did the action, which then read the file it was writing:\n`cat: comment.md: input file is output file`, and the first benchmark\ncomment never posted. The action now builds its body in a file of its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the rule family and the agent their own columns in the benchmark comment\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Post no comment for a run a newer push cancelled\n\nThe first benchmark comment on the pull request said every gate \"could not\nmeasure\". The run it came from had been cancelled by the next push, its\ngate jobs wrote the fallback report on their way out, and the report job,\non `always()`, posted the lot about a commit nobody was measuring any more.\nBoth workflows now gate their comment steps on `!cancelled()`: a broken\nharness still comments, a superseded run says nothing and leaves the\nprevious comment as it was.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1658c8109ee621d19bbad06f47048f068f19b4cc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/1658c8109ee621d19bbad06f47048f068f19b4cc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721","verification":{"payload":"tree 1658c8109ee621d19bbad06f47048f068f19b4cc\nparent 18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1789742265 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789742265 +0200\n\nMeasure whether the skills land, with an eval suite and a CI gate (#10)\n\n* Measure whether the skills land, with an eval suite and a CI gate\n\n`yarn verify` answers \"are the rules followed?\" for the rules a linter covers.\nNothing answered it for the ~75 that no linter can reach. `evals/` does: a task\nis a prompt that tempts an agent to break one rule, run through `claude -p` in a\nworktree from HEAD, graded on the diff it produced.\n\nThree files, three jobs. `tasks.ts` holds the prompts, what each expects, and the\ngrader. `trial.ts` owns isolation and the agent run. `run.ts` owns Langfuse — one\nsuite run is a dataset run, so the pass rate is a line over time rather than a\nnumber that scrolls away.\n\nTwo things make a score readable a month later. `--arm without-skills` strips the\nrules from the worktree, which is the only way to tell a skill that caused a pass\nfrom a model that already knew the rule. And every run records the conditions it\nwas produced under: the model is pinned, the CLI version, the runner, and the\nhost of `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` are stored beside the score, because a CLI upgrade\nthat moves one arm reads as a skill regression otherwise.\n\nThe workflow runs the three gates as a matrix on PRs that touch the rules\nthemselves, and stays informative: `run.ts` exits zero whatever the score, so a\nred gate never blocks a merge while n=1 makes it noisy. A failing job there is a\nbroken harness, not a regressed skill.\n\nEach rule file''s `impact:` is what a gate is worth, so four testing rules are\nre-tiered down to what they actually cost when broken.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stop paying for the same dependencies twelve times a pull request\n\nA pull request installed everything twelve times: once in each of the six jobs,\nand once more inside each of the six eval worktrees. The measured shape was\nabsurd — `verify` spent 20s installing to do 2s of work, `test` spent 61s to do\n10s.\n\nTwo fixes, one per layer.\n\nChromium is now cached. It was ~150 MB fetched fresh in both jobs that need it,\n41s and 21s against 10s and 9s of actual testing. The key is the lockfile hash,\nwhich is coarser than the Playwright version alone — an unrelated bump\nre-downloads the browser — but it is the only key that cannot go stale against\nthe installed `@playwright/test`. `browsers:install` still runs on a hit: it is\nidempotent, and `--with-deps` pulls OS packages a cache cannot hold.\n\nA trial''s worktree now symlinks the dependencies already installed beside it. It\nis a checkout of HEAD on the same machine, so its `node_modules` was identical to\nthe one the suite was running from, rebuilt from scratch for every trial — in CI,\nsix times in series inside billed job time. `yarn verify` and `yarn test` resolve\nthrough the link unchanged, nothing in a trial writes into `node_modules`, and\n`git worktree remove` unlinks it without touching the target. Proven by running\nboth suites inside a symlinked worktree: verify green, 7 files and 45 tests\ngreen, and the shared tree intact afterwards.\n\nThe fallback install kept for a suite running without its own dependencies now\nsays `--frozen-lockfile`. It said `--immutable`, which is Yarn Berry''s spelling:\nYarn 1.22 ignores it in silence, so the one install that had to be pinned to the\nlockfile was the only one that wasn''t.\n\nNeither job is serialised behind a shared setup job, which is what a \"build once\"\nanswer would have cost. There is no build to share here — `verify` is `tsc\n--noEmit`, `test` transforms its own sources, `e2e` builds its preview through\nPlaywright''s `webServer` — and shipping `node_modules` between jobs as an\nartifact is slower than installing it warm.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Name a gate after the skill it gates, and run only the gates a change touched\n\nA gate was named after a rule family — `skill-effects-gate`, `skill-rerender-gate`,\n`skill-modern-js-gate` — and a rule family matches no path on disk. So the\nworkflow could only filter on `.claude/skills/**` as a whole, and editing one\nreact rule ran the javascript gate too: six trials and real money to score rules\nnobody had changed.\n\nA gate is now a skill, spelled exactly as its directory: `react` and `javascript`\ntoday, the other three declared in `GATES` and waiting for a first task.\n`.claude/skills/react/**` in a diff and the react gate are the same word, which\nis the whole point — the `changes` job intersects the skill directories in the\ndiff with `yarn evals --list-gates` and builds its matrix from the result. One\nreact rule now runs one gate. A change to `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` or the harness\nstill runs all of them, because those move any gate, and a pull request that\ntouched no gated skill runs none.\n\n`--list-gates` exists so the workflow does not restate the gate list. `tasks.ts`\nstays the one place a gate is declared, which is the failure `alias.ts` and\ntsconfig `paths` already warn about in this repo: half a declaration wired, the\nother half rotting. The one exception is the `workflow_dispatch` dropdown, which\ncannot be computed and says so.\n\nThe gate is deliberately coarser than the rule now: a `react` run averages\neffects and re-render. Nothing diagnostic is lost, because a per-task score is\nnamed after the task''s `api` — `effects_gate`, `memoization_gate` — never after\nits gate, so each rule keeps its own line in Langfuse.\n\nThe cost of the rename is the run-level `pass_rate` history: a gate is a Langfuse\ndataset, and three datasets become two new ones. That history is three runs deep\ntoday and would only get more expensive to discard.\n\n`intentional-tests/` is not a gate. It is not one of the five skills `AGENTS.md`\ndeclares, and no task gates it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Let a broken harness turn the job red\n\nThe Evals run on 9ba6a14 reported success. Both gates had died before their first\ntrial, on a Langfuse 409: dataset item ids are unique per project across\ndatasets, so renaming a gate could not move its items into the new dataset. The\njob was green anyway, and the failure was found by a human reading the logs.\n\nThe cause is not the 409. `bash -e` takes a pipeline''s status from its last\ncommand, and the gate step pipes the suite through `tee` to build the step\nsummary. `tee` always succeeds, so nothing `run.ts` does with its exit code can\nreach the runner.\n\nThis is the exact inversion of what the file claims two comments earlier: a\nfailing job here is meant to mean a broken harness — a missing secret, a CLI that\nwould not install — and it meant nothing at all.\n\n`set -o pipefail` restores it, and the informative-not-blocking property survives\nuntouched, because it was never the shell''s doing: `run.ts` prints the pass rate\nand exits zero whatever the score. A red gate stays green; a harness that cannot\nrun goes red.\n\nThe `changes` job gets the same line. It pipes through `sed`, `sort` and `jq`,\nand a broken link there would have selected no gates at all — a suite that\nsilently scores nothing, reported as a pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give a rule one source, so removing it removes it\n\n`AGENTS.md` restated the React Compiler rule verbatim from\n`.claude/skills/react/references/rerender.md`: write plain components, no\nhand-written `useMemo`, `useCallback` or `memo`.\n\nThe strip that makes the `without-skills` arm removes `.claude/skills` and the\nSkills section of this file. It does not reach a rule restated under a different\nheading, so both arms read the copy, and `rerender-memo` — the task that gates\nexactly this rule — was comparing a model that had been told the rule against a\nmodel that had been told the rule. The same copy sits inside the criterion of\n`rerender-move-state-down`, which fails an answer that reaches for memoization.\nTwo of the five react tasks were measuring nothing.\n\nNothing is lost by deleting it. `vite.config.ts` wires `reactCompilerPreset()`\nin plain sight, and `react/SKILL.md` already tells an agent to check a rule''s\n`requires:` against `package.json` and the build config. A fact about how this\nrepository is built belongs in the file that decides it, where that check finds\nit in either arm; a rule belongs in the skill, where the strip can take it away.\n\nThe Skills section now says so, so the copy does not come back. It is stripped\nalong with the rest, so the statement itself cannot leak into an arm.\n\nThe remaining sections were checked against all eight tasks. Verticals, StyleX\nand the tier-by-filename rule gate nothing, and no skill states them — they are\nthis repository''s own conventions and stay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Grade what a diff cannot see, and stop reading one trial as a rate\n\nThree changes to the harness, entangled in `run.ts` and committed together.\n\n**A judge, for rules a diff cannot separate.** `modern-set-operations` forced it:\nthe agent read the rule''s `requires: ES2025`, read this repository''s `lib` floor\nof ES2023, concluded the rule did not apply and wrote an array `filter` — which\nis the right answer. An agent with no skills writes the same `filter` out of\nignorance. Identical diff, opposite reasoning, one score, and the evidence sat\nunread in the closing summary.\n\nSo a judged task carries a `criterion` in prose and `judge.ts` hands a model the\ncriterion, the prompt, the diff and the summary. The criterion lives in\n`tasks.ts`, not in a Langfuse form: a CI gate has to read its own score before\nthe job ends, and a criterion is the definition of the score — it must be\nreviewable in a pull request and changeable in the same commit as the rule it\nmeasures. It is falsifiable, and that was checked rather than assumed: given the\nreal summary it passes and cites the ES2025-versus-lib sentence; given the same\ndiff with a bland summary it fails it.\n\nThe judge is pinned separately from the subject (`claude-sonnet-5`) and travels\nbeside `model` in the run metadata, because two models move in this system. It\nruns in a scratch directory with tools, MCP and slash commands disabled, so it\ncannot confirm a criterion the agent never satisfied — and that isolation is also\nwhat makes it affordable: 148,068 tokens and $0.59 as a plain question, about\n3,000 and $0.012 with the flags. A judge that cannot answer writes `judge_error`\nand no gate score at all, never a zero.\n\n**A `typescript` gate,** with `derive-vs-decouple`: a presentational component\nwhose props tempt `Pick<Account, …>`. Judge-graded, because `Pick`, `Omit`, a\nwhole-object prop and a standalone type all render identically.\n\n**`--repeat`, default 2.** A single trial is a coin flipped once. Its score is 1\nor 0 and says nothing about how repeatable it is: a gate reading 5/5 one week and\n4/5 the next has moved by roughly one standard deviation of a five-task run whose\ntrue pass probability is 0.8. Neither number is wrong and neither answers the\nquestion.\n\nEach task now runs `repeat` times and its score is the mean, so a task that\npasses once in two reads 0.5 instead of whichever trial happened to be graded.\n`pass_rate` is the mean of those means, so a task cannot count twice for having\nbeen sampled twice. Two is the smallest count that can disagree with itself;\nraise it for the tasks whose arms sit within a point of each other, which is\nprecisely what repetition answers, and drop it to 1 to exercise the harness.\n\nThe repetition index joins the model and the arm in the stored-trial key, for the\nsame reason they are there: without it the second trial overwrites the first and\n`--reuse` grades one sample as if it were several. `--reuse` counts a task as\nstored only when every repetition the run asks for is on disk. `repeat` travels\nin the run metadata — a pass rate read without knowing how many samples it\naverages says nothing about how much of its movement is noise.\n\n`trial_cost_usd` now sums a task''s repetitions, and `judge_error` carries how\nmany of them the judge swallowed, so a mean over fewer samples than the run asked\nfor says so. `TrialOutcome` loses its `skipped` variant: `runTrial` never\nproduced one, `run.ts` did, and skipping is a property of a dataset item rather\nthan of a trial.\n\nThe workflow gains a `repeat` input and 30 more minutes: the largest gate is now\nten trials rather than five. A gate costs double what it did.\n\n`docs/lint-coverage.md` maps every rule to what can check it — lint, diff grader,\nor judge — and is what the remaining tasks are written from. It stays untracked,\nand `.gitignore` says why: a trial''s worktree is a checkout of HEAD, so a tracked\ncopy would put the answer key in front of the agent being measured. In both arms\nat once, which is not a difference between them but a floor raised under both.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run a gate three tasks abreast, and stop scoring a rate limit as a regression\n\nThe react gate is five tasks run one after another, twice each, at one to\nfour minutes a trial: the wall clock of every evals run was the sum of them.\n`run.ts` takes `--max-concurrency` and CI passes three. Tasks, not trials —\na task''s repetitions stay serial, so the samples one score averages never\nrace each other for the same token. Nothing about the cost moves: the same\ntrials spend the same tokens, only closer together.\n\nCloser together is also when a shared token hits its window, and until now\na trial `claude -p` could not finish graded as a wrong answer. There is no\n`failed` any more. An agent that answered badly is `completed` with a bad\ndiff; a trial the harness could not obtain — a rate limit, the timeout, a\nCLI that would not start — is `unavailable`, writes `trial_error` and no\ngate score, the treatment `judge_error` already had. One non-zero exit is\nstill an answer: out of `--max-turns` leaves a diff, and the diff is graded.\n\nThe workflow''s matrix is now gate × arm, but a pull request only pays for\nthe arm it can move. `without-skills` strips the skill before the agent\nstarts, so a skill edit re-measures a constant there; it runs on a pull\nrequest only when the baseline itself changed — the prompts, the pinned\nmodel, the CLI pin, `AGENTS.md` — and otherwise waits for a manual run. The\nmanual inputs say `all` and `both` where they used to say nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the effort the subject and the judge think at, as the model already is\n\n`claude -p` reads `effortLevel` from the settings of whichever machine runs\nit. A laptop and a CI runner can disagree, a trial that thinks harder passes\nmore often, and the difference reads as a skill regression — the same\nfailure an unpinned model produced, on a variable nobody had named. `--effort`\nnow travels to the subject''s `claude -p`, `--judge-effort` to the judge''s,\nand both land in the run metadata beside `model` and `judgeModel`.\n\nBoth default to `high`. It is Opus 5''s own default, so the line does not\nstep. The judge stays there on purpose: it costs a cent, and a verdict that\nflips on a borderline diff costs a gate score.\n\nEffort joins the cache key. Stored trials from before carry no level in\ntheir name and are no longer served by `--reuse`: which level they ran at is\nnot known, and a guess in a filename is the false number the key exists to\nprevent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Change config eval\n\n* Put the agent under test behind one interface, and wire Codex and Copilot to it\n\nEverything below the CLI call was already agent-agnostic — the worktree, the\ndiff, the graders and the Langfuse reporting never ask who wrote the change.\nOnly three things were Claude''s: the command and the shape of its result,\nwhere the agent reads the rules from, and whether it takes a reasoning\neffort. Those are what an `Agent` now carries, and `agents.ts` is the only\nfile that knows a CLI exists. `--agent` names who is measured, and\n`--list-agents` is where CI reads the names from rather than repeating them.\n\nAn `Agent` declares what its CLI takes, and `run.ts` refuses what it does not:\n`--effort` against an agent with no efforts, `--max-turns` against one with no\nturn cap, are command-line errors rather than flags quietly dropped. Nothing\nis recorded that was not applied — the rule the model and effort pins already\nobeyed, now enforced for every agent.\n\nCodex is driven through `codex exec`, Copilot through `copilot -p`. Neither\nhas been run: both were written against the documented flags, and the pinned\nmodel ids are the ones this suite intends to measure, not ones confirmed to\nexist on any account. A wrong pin fails the trial rather than quietly\nmeasuring something else.\n\nFour things do not port, and the shape of the code says so. Where the rules\nlive: Claude pulls a skill in on demand, so the two arms are not the same\ntreatment across agents — the with/without delta travels, the absolute pass\nrate does not. Effort and turns: a flag on Claude, a config override on Codex,\nand on Copilot a settings-file key that cannot be set for one run without also\nsupplying that CLI''s model and MCP config, so Copilot declares no efforts at\nall. Cost: Claude reports dollars, Codex tokens, Copilot nothing — the flag\nthat leaves the final message alone on stdout is the one that strips the\nstats — and an agent reporting no cost publishes no `trial_cost_usd`, because\na zero reads as a free run. Saying it failed: Claude has a `subtype` and Codex\na `turn.failed` event, so both can tell a trial the harness could not obtain\nfrom an agent that answered badly; Copilot offers only an exit code, so a run\nthat fails partway and still exits zero is scored as an answer. That hole is\ndocumented where it lives and needs a structured output mode to close.\n\nCI grows an agent axis, opt-in because it multiplies against the gates and the\narms. An `evals:<agent>` label selects who runs, no label means the default\nalone, and a label naming an agent that does not exist fails the run rather\nthan quietly scoring the default. Every job still installs the Claude CLI: the\njudge stays Claude whichever agent is under test, because it is the\ninstrument, not the subject.\n\nBefore a Codex or Copilot score means anything, the rules have to reach them.\nThe Skills section of `AGENTS.md` names Claude''s Skill tool, which Codex has\nnot; Copilot does not read `.claude/skills` at all. Until that is fixed, both\narms hand them the same context and a near-zero delta is that gap rather than\na rule that stopped earning its place.\n\nAlso flips the `requires:` on the memoized-components rule to React Compiler\noff, which is what the rule is for.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Ask each task in several voices, and key a trial by the prompt it was given\n\nA task carried one prompt, so a rule that survived only the phrasing it was\nwritten against scored the same as one that held up. `prompts` is a list now,\none trial per entry, and `--repeat` is the number of passes over that list\nrather than the number of trials: a task with one prompt is unchanged at two\ntrials, and a second phrasing costs two more.\n\nThe prompt index joins the cache key, beside the agent, arm, model, effort and\nrepetition already there. Without it the second variant would overwrite the\nfirst on disk, and `--reuse` would grade one sample under metadata claiming\nseveral — the false-precision failure the rest of that key exists to prevent.\nFor the same reason `cachedTaskIds` counts a task as stored only when every\nprompt and every repetition it would run is present.\n\nThe Langfuse item carries the whole list as its input, and each trial is judged\nagainst the prompt it was actually given rather than the task''s first one.\n\nThe grader read back off a dataset item now parses through a Zod schema. That\nitem was written by an earlier run, possibly under an older shape, so it is\nuntrusted input rather than a literal `tsc` has checked; the hand-rolled\nfield-by-field check it replaces stated the diff families a second time, and\nonly one of the two was ever going to be updated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Ask the repository what to measure, and the agent whether it read the rule\n\nThree changes to the harness, one seam and two things it made visible.\n\n**The seam.** `tasks.ts` held both halves of this suite: what a task *is* and\nwhat this repository''s tasks *are*. The second is content — prompts, rules,\nfamilies, what counts as an answer — and the first is machinery that knows\nnothing about React or `src/`. They are split down `evals.config.ts`, at the\nroot beside `vite.config.ts` and `vitest.config.ts`: `evals/grading.ts` says\nwhat a task looks like and how a counted answer is scored, `evals/config.ts`\nloads the config and checks it, and `tasks.ts` is repository content that sits\nin `evals/` only because 300 lines of prompt prose do not belong in a config\nfile.\n\nThe config holds two keys and the shortness is the point. Arms, agents, models,\neffort, repetition and concurrency are flags with a default in `run.ts`, and a\nkey beside a flag would be a second place a run''s conditions get decided — the\nhalf-wired failure `alias.ts` and tsconfig `paths` warn about. There is no\n`--config` either: a suite that can be pointed at two of them is a suite whose\nscores were produced under conditions nobody can read off the run.\n\n`sourcePaths` has no default, and `src/**` is wider than the file types that\nexist today. Both for one reason: a whitelist that misses a real answer scores\nit 0 rather than showing nothing, and a default that missed a repository''s\nsources would empty every diff and score every task 0 — a plausible, false\nnumber, which is the one thing this suite exists not to produce. The globs are\nprefixed `:(glob)` where they reach git, because a bare pathspec reads a double\nstar as a single one and would have graded one directory level.\n\nThe diff grader''s `added` pattern moves onto the task for the same reason the\nprompts did. The fixed list of families it replaces had four entries and two\nwere dead, left behind by tasks that had since become judged. `run.ts` is\nchecked against the config it loads only where a type cannot speak: that the\narrays hold something, and that a pattern written for a grader is one the\ngrader can compile rather than one that throws mid-run.\n\n**The strip stops asking who is reading.** `Agent` carried a `stripRules`,\non the belief that each CLI finds its rules somewhere else. All three discover\na skill the same way — by its description, pulling the body in only once they\ncommit — and what differs is which directories they scan. So the\n\"without-skills\" arm now empties `.claude/skills`, `.github/skills` and\n`.agents/skills` and takes no agent at all. Emptying a directory this\nrepository never filled costs nothing, and it means a skill later dropped into\none nobody thought to strip cannot leak into the arm that is meant to be\nwithout them.\n\nThe appended scoped-exercise line stops naming `yarn verify` and `yarn test`\nwith it. What a project''s checks are called is in its `AGENTS.md`, which the\nagent has already read.\n\n**A gate score was two hypotheses in one number.** A `*_gate` that moves is\neither the rule that stopped working or the agent that never reached for it,\nand the evidence sat unread in tool calls. The Claude adapter reads the event\nstream rather than the single object `--output-format json` returns, collects\nthe `Skill` invocations, and each trial carries the skills it loaded.\n`skill_invoked` is the mean over the trials whose agent can report — undefined\nis a CLI that cannot say, never a zero, because a zero standing in for the\ndifference reads as a skill that never fires. On `without-skills` the score\nbelongs at 0 and is the strip verifying itself: anything above it means the two\narms were never the two treatments the run claims. The stream costs no tokens;\nit decides what the CLI prints locally, not what is sent.\n\n**`--gate` is required.** It defaulted to `react`, which runs one skill out of\nfive and reports its pass rate as the suite''s. A named `--task` still settles\nthe gate on its own, and a repository with one gate has nothing to choose. A\ngate is also declared only by a task naming it now — a skill nobody wrote a\ntask for is not a gate — and a `--gate` with no skill directory behind it is a\ncommand-line error rather than a stack trace mid-run.\n\nCI adds `evals.config.ts` to both lists it appears in: the paths that trigger\nthe suite, and the ones that move a baseline and so re-run both arms.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Remove local harnais\n\n* Add eleven tasks for the unlinted CRITICAL and HIGH rules\n\ndocs/eval-candidates.md extracts, from the oxlint-config coverage doc, every\nskill rule no linter can measure and says whether its eval should count a\ncall or ask a judge. The eleven tasks are the CRITICAL and HIGH rows of it that\nhave no lint half at all and are not React Native, each with two prompt\nvoices.\n\nSeven of them are judged where the doc said grader: avoid-hasty-abstraction,\neffects-fetch-race-condition, rerender-children-as-props and the four\nperf-async rules. In each the right and wrong answers add the same lines and\ndiffer only in which function encloses a call or where an await sits. The\nthree diff graders — a literal import() specifier, an untouched shared\nhandlers file read off the diff header, and an assertion on the msw store —\nwere exercised against sample diff lines.\n\nTwo harness changes came with them. The trial now runs git add\n--intent-to-add before diffing, because git diff HEAD skips untracked files\nand a new component, locale module or test file never reached the grader.\nAnd server/ is graded as well as src/, since three javascript tasks change\nthe API process.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Put the judge behind an interface, and let the repository choose it\n\nThe judge was one pinned `claude -p` call welded into the grader. It is now an\nadapter like the agent, with `--judge-agent`, so a machine without the Claude\nCLI can run a judged gate — and so the one bias this suite could not see from\ninside can be measured: a Claude judge reading a Claude subject.\n\nA narrower interface than `Agent`, not a reuse of it. An agent is handed a\nrepository and every tool; a judge must have neither, and what buys that is\nper-CLI: the isolation flags, how a system prompt is supplied at all, and the\nshape the answer comes back in.\n\nTwo judges are wired, both probed against the installed CLI. Copilot is pinned\nto `gpt-5.4` for the reason that matters: its own default is `claude-sonnet-5`,\nso an unpinned Copilot judge would grade a Claude subject with Claude while\nappearing on the chart to be a second vendor. Codex stays absent — `codex exec`\nhas no system-prompt flag and no verified isolation to pair with one, and a\njudge adapter that is wrong moves every score in the dataset quietly, where a\nsubject adapter that is wrong fails its own trial visibly.\n\nThe same probes falsified three things the Copilot *agent* adapter claimed, and\nthose are fixed with it: `--reasoning-effort` is a flag rather than a settings\nkey, `--output-format json` is a structured mode, and `copilot skill --help`\nnames `.claude/skills` among its discovery paths — so its `with-skills` arm was\na real comparison all along. The adapter now reports which skills loaded, and a\nrun that fails partway is `unavailable` rather than a zero.\n\n`evals.config.ts` grows from three keys to seven. `defaultJudge` mirrors\n`defaultAgent`; `agents` and `judges` hold the model and the effort, keyed by\nthe CLI they belong to because a model id is a string one CLI knows and another\nhas never heard of; `maxConcurrency` is the machine''s speed rather than the\nexperiment''s shape. Every one of them is a chain — flag, then this file, then\nthe adapter''s pin — and every one lands in the run metadata, which is the test a\nkey has to pass to live there.\n\nTwo steps in the line, both deliberate. This repository now grades with Copilot\nrather than Claude, so no verdict from here is comparable to one from before.\nAnd the Copilot agent''s effort is pinned at `high` against the CLI''s own\n`medium`. `--repeat` defaults to 1: the run people actually type asks the cheap\nquestion, and the run that wants a measurement asks for the samples.\n\nCI follows `defaultJudge` rather than restating it: the Copilot CLI is installed\nin every job because the judge needs it, and each agent''s own CLI only where it\nis under test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Install the rules under test instead of inheriting them\n\nEvery eval job failed in under a second on `No skill directory at\n.claude/skills/react`, and the failure was the harness behaving correctly.\n`96327bc` took `.claude/skills/` out of this repository; the rules are a Claude\nplugin published from another one now, and a worktree cut from this one holds\nnone of them.\n\nThe silence behind that error is the real problem. The plugin installs against a\nproject path and is enabled by that project''s settings, so an agent running in\n`/tmp/eval-…` has no rules at all — and a `with-skills` arm measuring an agent\nwith no rules scores like the arm that removed them. The gate would have read\n\"this skill does nothing\", which is the plausible, false number this suite exists\nto refuse. It failed loudly instead, which is the only reason anyone noticed.\n\nSo the harness stops inheriting the rules and installs them. `skills.pin.json`\nnames the repository, the ref and the directory inside it. `skills.ts` clones it\nonce into gitignored `evals/.skills/`, resolves the ref to a commit, and fills\neach worktree''s `.claude/skills/` from that commit with `git archive`. A trial\nthen holds everything the agent read, which is what makes it a measurement\nrather than an anecdote, and the commit travels in the metadata so a score says\nwhich text produced it.\n\nFour choices in there are load-bearing, and each was measured rather than\nassumed:\n\n- **Not the clone `claude plugin` leaves in `~/.claude/plugins`.** It is shallow\n  — one commit, verified — so it can serve neither a pin other than its tip nor\n  any baseline; the CLI moves it, so it can change under a ninety-minute run;\n  and it is where a rule is authored, so it may hold uncommitted edits.\n- **`git archive <rev>:<path>`, the tree-ish form.** The two-argument form emits\n  entries under the full path, and extracting that into `.claude/skills` would\n  bury every rule three directories deep, where no agent looks and no error is\n  raised.\n- **The gates and no more, though the plugin ships eleven skills.** An extra one\n  is not neutral: `react-native` describes itself in terms of re-renders and\n  performance, which is what three of the `react` gate''s tasks are about.\n- **The rules hash keys `with-skills` and not `without-skills`.** That arm\n  installs nothing, so the revision is not one of its inputs; keying it would\n  discard the one arm worth caching every time a rule nobody ablated moved.\n\nThe pin is its own JSON file rather than a key in `evals.config.ts`, because\nbumping it is how a rule change now reaches this repository and the workflow\ntreats a config edit as a change to what a trial *is* — every gate in both arms.\nA rule edit moves one arm only. CI grows a branch for the pin, a step that clones\nthe rules with a deploy key, and loses the `sed` that derived touched gates from\na path this repository no longer has.\n\nProven on live trials rather than by inspection: the same task scores\n`skill_invoked` 1.000 with the rules installed and 0.000 in the ablated arm. That\ndelta is what was silently missing, and it is the check to run after touching any\nof this.\n\n`--arm previous-skill` leaves the CI matrix. This repository''s diff can show that\nthe pin moved but not which rule moved with it, and only the skills repository''s\nown history can say; until its pipeline drives this workflow, that arm is manual.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Clone the rules with a scoped token rather than an SSH key\n\nAzure DevOps has no per-repository deploy keys. An SSH key there belongs to an\naccount and carries everything that account can do, across the whole\norganisation, writes included — and a secret on this repository is readable by\nanyone who can edit a workflow in it. That is a credential the eval jobs do not\nneed: they read one directory of one repository.\n\nA PAT scoped to Code (Read) is the narrowest thing Azure DevOps can issue, so\nthat is what the clone step now takes, over https.\n\nThe pin keeps its SSH url, because that is what a developer''s own machine clones\nwith, and the https url is derived from it in the step rather than configured\nbeside it — the pin stays the one place the repository is named, and a\nmalformed url fails the job with a message saying so rather than cloning\nnothing.\n\nThe token reaches git through the credential helper''s file, not the url, so it\nis never on a command line, in the process table, or in a line git echoes back\nwhen a clone fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say which clone, and which ref, when the rules cannot be read\n\nA CI job failed with `unknown revision or path not in the working tree`, naming\nthe pinned ref. That message was the second of two attempts and the first was\nswallowed, so it described a ref that does exist upstream while saying nothing\nabout the clone it was looked for in.\n\nThree fixes, all of them about where an error is raised rather than what fails:\n\n- `EVALS_SKILLS_DIR` is checked to be a clone before it is used. A path that is\n  not one produced a worktree with no rules in it, which is the failure this\n  module exists to make impossible.\n- Both resolution attempts are reported. `origin/<ref>` failing and `<ref>`\n  failing say different things — an unfetched branch is not a typo — and the\n  message now carries the commands to tell them apart.\n- The clone step verifies its own result: it prints the commit count and the\n  remote branches, and fails naming the missing branch. The answer is there, not\n  three steps later.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the commit the rules were merged into, not the branch that carried them\n\nThe branch `evals/skills.pin.json` named was merged into `master` and deleted\nupstream. A local clone kept a stale remote-tracking ref, so the pin resolved\nhere and nowhere else — which is how a CI job came to report a revision that\ndoes not exist while the same command worked on a laptop.\n\nPinned to the commit rather than to `master`, and the distinction is the whole\ndesign: with a branch, a rule changes under a run with nothing in this\nrepository to show it, and the workflow branch that exists to catch a rule\nchange never fires. A commit makes a rule change arrive as an edit to this file,\nwhich is an event CI can see.\n\nNothing measured moves. The merged commit carries byte-identical rules — the\ntree hash over the five gates is `0d8318a2c77a` before and after — so the stored\ntrials keyed by it stay valid.\n\nThe clone step''s check now accepts both forms, as `skills.ts` does: a branch has\nno local head in a clone nobody checks out, and a commit has no `origin/` form.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Measure Copilot by default, and have it graded on Sonnet rather than by itself\n\nThe people these skills are written for run Copilot, so the harness now\ndefaults to it as the subject and the judge alike, and evals.config.ts stops\nrepeating a value equal to the default. The workflow_dispatch dropdown agrees.\n\nGrading gpt-5.4 with gpt-5.4 would have made the judge''s self-reading bias\ninvisible, so the Copilot judge is pinned to claude-sonnet-5: the other vendor\nthrough the CLI every CI job already installs. A claude subject under this\njudge is Claude reading Claude, and the README says to pass\n--judge-model gpt-5.4 for that run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Fail the job, and keep no run, when a verdict could not be reached\n\nThe workflow said a red job meant a broken harness, but run.ts always exited\nzero, so a Copilot token the CLI rejected left every trial unavailable and the\njob green. Now any verdict the run could not reach — a trial that would not\nrun, a judge that would not answer, a judged task cut off before its summary —\nexits non-zero. A trial that ran and failed still exits zero: that is a result.\n\nSuch a run is deleted from the Langfuse dataset rather than kept with its\ntrial_error counts, so the chart holds only runs that measured. The reasons\nstay in the report run.ts prints, which CI copies into the job summary, and\nthe traces stay with what the failed trials cost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Score every task as rule_gate, and let Langfuse mean it into the pass rate\n\nScores were named after the grader''s family, so five gates already put eleven\nmostly empty columns in the Langfuse runs list, and the fifteen families this\nrepository is heading for would have made it unreadable. Every task now writes\nthe one score rule_gate; the family goes in front of the comment and into the\nitem''s metadata, where it groups tasks without becoming a column.\n\npass_rate goes with it: with one score name, the Ø rule_gate column Langfuse\ncomputes over a run''s items is that same mean, and a second copy written by\nrun.ts said nothing more.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Revert \"Score every task as rule_gate, and let Langfuse mean it into the pass rate\"\n\nThis reverts commit f02d69b. The per-family columns are the level that will\nmatter once a family holds more than a handful of tasks, and Langfuse offers no\ngrouping by item metadata to get them back; a score named after the family is\nthe only way it shows them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Regroup the javascript and typescript families under the rules they test\n\n`sorting` and `sets` were two families for one rule, the modern-API one, and\n`types` named a gate rather than the derive-versus-decouple rule its only task\ngrades. Renamed to `modern` and `derive`, so a family reads as the rule it\nbelongs to. The scores move with them: `sorting_gate`, `sets_gate` and\n`types_gate` stop being written, and runs recorded under those names are no\nlonger comparable to what comes next.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read a verdict out of a reply that is not clean JSON, and delete the run by the name Langfuse stored\n\nThe first run to lose a verdict showed both holes. The Copilot judge on Sonnet\nanswered with a JSON object the parser rejected — a paragraph in `reason`, most\nlikely, which puts a raw newline inside a string — and the reply was recorded\n200 characters long, too short to say. The verdict is now read out of the reply\nby its `passed` boolean when the whole does not parse, and a reply that carries\nno verdict travels whole in the reason.\n\nAnd the deletion that should have followed asked for a run that did not exist:\nthe SDK stores `${name} - ${timestamp}` unless told the run name, so the run\nstayed on the chart with a 404 in the job log. The name is now passed verbatim.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Score real pull requests online, with the same graders and score names\n\n`run.ts` gives an agent an exercise and grades the answer, which says\nwhether a rule is well written. Nothing said whether the agents people\nactually use follow it on real work. `evals/online.ts` does: it takes the\nsource diff of a branch from its merge base, runs each task''s `online`\ngrader over it, and reports one Langfuse trace tagged `online` whose\nscores carry the `<family>_gate` names the offline tier writes, so the\ntwo meet in the Scores view under one name.\n\nA task''s own grader cannot read an arbitrary diff: its criterion names\nthe component the prompt pointed at, and a diff grader that expects\n`some` asks for what no pull request can be told to contain. So a task\nthat wants to be measured online carries a second grader: a `forbidden`\nsignature counted on added lines, or a `criterion` that first says when\nit applies. The judge returns `applies` before `passed`, and a \"no\" is\nno score rather than a pass. Eleven tasks carry one.\n\n`diff.ts` now owns the one definition of the diff a grader reads;\n`trial.ts` uses it too. The trace records which coding agents signed\nthe commits, from their trailers and accounts, as a filter rather than\na gate: every change is scored, whoever wrote it.\n\n`online.yml` runs it on every pull request, report-only. Whether the\njudge runs is `onlineJudge` in `evals.config.ts`, false for now: the\nonline criteria are new, and one already needed tightening after a\nsmoke test, so their verdicts are read against real diffs before they\nare trusted. `--judge on|off` wins over the key locally.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read the online judge''s reply the same tolerant way, through one reader with tests\n\nThe tolerant reading added in 64d922c went into one of two copies of the\nparser: the online judge kept the strict `JSON.parse`, so a paragraph in\n`reason` still lost the verdict when scoring a real pull request, with 200\ncharacters of the reply to explain it. Both parsers now go through one\n`readReply`, which reads `applies`, `passed` and `reason` out of the text\nwhen the whole does not parse, and keeps 2000 characters when it finds no\nverdict at all.\n\nThe reading is a pure function and now has unit tests, including the exact\nreply that broke the first run. `evals/` had no test tier, so the unit\nproject now includes it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Post each workflow''s report as one comment on the pull request, edited in place\n\nThe scores lived in the job summary and in Langfuse, neither of which a\nreviewer opens from the conversation. Each workflow now owns one comment on\nthe pull request, found again by a marker and edited on every push, so the\nreport of a superseded commit does not pile up under the review.\n\nThe online tier posts as soon as it has scored. The offline tier posts once\nevery gate has finished: each gate keeps its report as an artifact, and a\n`report` job after the matrix gathers them into one comment, rather than ten\ncomments on an `AGENTS.md` edit. Both run on `always()`, because a gate that\nbroke is the report most worth reading and its reasons are in what `run.ts`\nprinted before it exited.\n\nThe posting is a composite action both workflows use, so the lookup and the\nedit are written once.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Write the pull request comment for the developer whose pull request it is\n\nThe comment was the harness''s own report in a code block, under the names\nthe harness uses for itself. A developer reading their pull request does not\nknow an arm from a gate, and the number they wanted was three screens down.\n\nEach run now also writes what it found as data, `--report-file`, and a\nrenderer turns it into the comment: one line per rule, a verdict and one\nsentence of why, and the links to Langfuse and to the job. The offline tier\nis \"Skill benchmark\", one line per rule family with a column per arm; the\nonline tier is \"Rules check\", failures first. The names the workflows show\nsay what they measure; the names in the code still say how.\n\nA pull request with no source file in its diff no longer gets a comment\nsaying so: a first job asks `--list-files`, and the scoring job is skipped,\nwhich reads as \"not about this\" on the pull request rather than as a pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the rules check as one job, and say nothing when there is nothing to grade\n\nThe job that asked \"anything to check?\" ahead of the scoring one read badly\non the pull request, two checks where a developer expects one. The check is\none job again, as it was. A diff with no source file in it still gets no\ncomment: `online.ts` exits zero without a report in that case, and the\ncomment step reads a green job with no report as nothing to say.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the comment action its own temp file\n\nBoth workflows name their body file `comment.md` under the runner''s temp\ndirectory, and so did the action, which then read the file it was writing:\n`cat: comment.md: input file is output file`, and the first benchmark\ncomment never posted. The action now builds its body in a file of its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the rule family and the agent their own columns in the benchmark comment\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Post no comment for a run a newer push cancelled\n\nThe first benchmark comment on the pull request said every gate \"could not\nmeasure\". The run it came from had been cancelled by the next push, its\ngate jobs wrote the fallback report on their way out, and the report job,\non `always()`, posted the lot about a commit nobody was measuring any more.\nBoth workflows now gate their comment steps on `!cancelled()`: a broken\nharness still comments, a superseded run says nothing and leaves the\nprevious comment as it was.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrUy5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVp4QAF5gwh/Zg4up2rLTxuz2GWAd\nvIT+Cg0uD32SMhGJHEvkiHq5jViVB2G5mT5UT3oIgkZC+0zrTVAA9ZsZjFj7t9sh\nnLW/hcQvInbz2/EqGLZa4yV0+bCHvpO9JTd7p2pPJL6jBmcx7tR6wMLxvSmd0RZe\n2mwpE4K3MLgqEy/cmpepgSVMlh44gxuyMCjnsM78QpE/1T5DWT0zvdaW/oUkaYoH\ny0sa/A1shZr/0YANlEL1DhDpRWu7co0QuZB1Xou+cg0Qm/gnDYGvVn9l6rTC0/7D\ni/P9q46+ja2NysN9BeXaek6jCdjNK4aVfoYeGpK3rIrzDOu7BA0OvZDsfqa4ecsF\n7MrCd9K+tWku2fyUxgJjf2Ex9iAIDJTqcUAOteWCAfdl0pMrEuHhMI6usE5/9CPT\nL8Bfb/ayXxmZgkn+4EHHeewrWmYrNM2N0ScnDGGzJbDscAKQ4eY5IrMPXlBkeCuB\nuEkE6SMcX7n/hhpwMhLs88VuaqOikZ2mM1+O+VahNb7GHLAX2XmPlyqC/eg7jv6R\n5DrTO4JPE12oAiFUrwwgcxC0EF0fdgF7tw9dHDH2K9raRzIzL9XTf4JzARrV0r9B\nIeP09u9PBP78HMrsYqjCZ7ViJ4FnyUva3spk3ipOjdWnR9vhyJTV5NdaWTQ+bEAF\nEasRq4auE1uc7xkPdQaL\n=E6LQ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T14:37:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721","node_id":"C_kwDOTtvMrdoAKGFiNmNhOWU4MmM0MGEwY2FkMTQ5Nzk3MTNjZmUyZTc4YTRkZDE3MjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commit/18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30","sha":"18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30"}],"sha":"ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/ab6ca9e82c40a0cad14979713cfe2e78a4dd1721"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/305502597?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/aregnaud-newforma","id":305502597,"login":"aregnaud-newforma","node_id":"U_kgDOEjWZhQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aregnaud-newforma","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/commits/18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-04T12:00:07Z","email":"aregnaud@newforma.com","name":"aregnaud-newforma"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-04T12:00:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Declare the browser floor, and let each rule declare what it needs (#9)\n\n* chore: declare the browser floor instead of inheriting it\n\nVite 8 defaults `build.target` to `baseline-widely-available`, which resolves\nto chrome111/edge111/firefox114/safari16.4/ios16.4 — pinned per Vite major, so\na Vite upgrade would move which browsers this app supports with nothing in the\ndiff to show it. Nothing in the repository said which browsers those were.\n\nWrite that floor down and make the three places that had an opinion agree:\n\n- `package.json` `browserslist`, for every tool that reads it.\n- `vite.config.ts` `build.target`, restated in esbuild''s vocabulary because\n  Vite does not read `browserslist` and ignores it in silence.\n- `tsconfig.json` `lib`/`target`, raised ES2021 → ES2023, which is what those\n  browsers actually ship.\n- `.oxlintrc.json` `env`, lowered es2024 → es2023, where the linter was\n  declaring globals the shipped browsers do not have.\n\nThe values are what the build already did, so no behaviour changes and the\nbundle is byte-identical. What changes is that `lib` now sits exactly on the\nfloor, which makes `tsc` the gate: `Set.prototype.difference` is refused with\n\"Try changing the ''lib'' compiler option to ''es2025'' or later\", where before it\nwas accepted by nobody and caught by nobody. Raising `lib` from here means\nraising the floor first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: let each rule declare the configuration it needs\n\nA skill rule is not universally applicable. `modern-set-operations` needs\nES2025; `rerender-memo-props-stability` only holds with the React Compiler off,\nand contradicts `rerender-memo`, which only holds with it on. Until now that\nlived solely in the coverage table, where the agent applying the rule never\nlooks — so a rule out of reach for a repository read as a rule to apply, and\nthe failure only surfaced as a type error, or not at all.\n\nAdd `requires:` to the frontmatter of the 26 rules that carry a precondition:\n\n- the 15 `modern-*` rules, each with the ECMAScript edition its API landed in,\n  from ES2023 (`toSorted`) to ES2027 (`using`), taken from TC39''s finished\n  proposals rather than from the TypeScript lib that happens to declare them;\n- 11 React rules, with `React Compiler on`/`off` or `React >=19`/`>=19.2`.\n\n\"Type-aware\" is deliberately not among them: it says which linter can catch a\nrule, not whether the rule applies to the code, and mixing the two would make\nthe field unreadable.\n\nA field nothing reads is decoration, so both hubs now say what to do with it.\n`modern.md` sends the reader to `tsconfig.json` `lib` first and says a rule\nabove it does not apply here. `react/SKILL.md` gains a Preconditions section\nthat also states why the compiler pair contradicts itself on purpose.\n\nThis stays advisory. The gate is `tsc`, which refuses an API above `lib`;\n`requires:` is what stops an agent from writing the code, hitting TS2550 and\nstarting over.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fdb5b6a687d4de862c4f9bb3b9e7e6580ed4788c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/trees/fdb5b6a687d4de862c4f9bb3b9e7e6580ed4788c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aregnaud-newforma/frontend-demo-app/git/commits/18d8539d7fe972583867d72544f2622268867a30","verification":{"payload":"tree fdb5b6a687d4de862c4f9bb3b9e7e6580ed4788c\nparent e7a5e5a5e9913d27928361b22fd81f9965580547\nauthor aregnaud-newforma <aregnaud@newforma.com> 1788523207 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788523207 +0200\n\nDeclare the browser floor, and let each rule declare what it needs (#9)\n\n* chore: declare the browser floor instead of inheriting it\n\nVite 8 defaults `build.target` to `baseline-widely-available`, which resolves\nto chrome111/edge111/firefox114/safari16.4/ios16.4 — pinned per Vite major, so\na Vite upgrade would move which browsers this app supports with nothing in the\ndiff to show it. Nothing in the repository said which browsers those were.\n\nWrite that floor down and make the three places that had an opinion agree:\n\n- `package.json` `browserslist`, for every tool that reads it.\n- `vite.config.ts` `build.target`, restated in esbuild''s vocabulary because\n  Vite does not read `browserslist` and ignores it in silence.\n- `tsconfig.json` `lib`/`target`, raised ES2021 → ES2023, which is what those\n  browsers actually ship.\n- `.oxlintrc.json` `env`, lowered es2024 → es2023, where the linter was\n  declaring globals the shipped browsers do not have.\n\nThe values are what the build already did, so no behaviour changes and the\nbundle is byte-identical. What changes is that `lib` now sits exactly on the\nfloor, which makes `tsc` the gate: `Set.prototype.difference` is refused with\n\"Try changing the ''lib'' compiler option to ''es2025'' or later\", where before it\nwas accepted by nobody and caught by nobody. Raising `lib` from here means\nraising the floor first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: let each rule declare the configuration it needs\n\nA skill rule is not universally applicable. `modern-set-operations` needs\nES2025; `rerender-memo-props-stability` only holds with the React Compiler off,\nand contradicts `rerender-memo`, which only holds with it on. Until now that\nlived solely in the coverage table, where the agent applying the rule never\nlooks — so a rule out of reach for a repository read as a rule to apply, and\nthe failure only surfaced as a type error, or not at all.\n\nAdd `requires:` to the frontmatter of the 26 rules that carry a precondition:\n\n- the 15 `modern-*` rules, each with the ECMAScript edition its API landed in,\n  from ES2023 (`toSorted`) to ES2027 (`using`), taken from TC39''s finished\n  proposals rather than from the TypeScript lib that happens to declare them;\n- 11 React rules, with `React Compiler on`/`off` or `React >=19`/`>=19.2`.\n\n\"Type-aware\" is deliberately not among them: it says which linter can catch a\nrule, not whether the rule applies to the code, and mixing the two would make\nthe field unreadable.\n\nA field nothing reads is decoration, so both hubs now say what to do with it.\n`modern.md` sends the reader to `tsconfig.json` `lib` first and says a rule\nabove it does not apply here. `react/SKILL.md` gains a Preconditions section\nthat also states why the compiler pair contradicts itself on purpose.\n\nThis stays advisory. The gate is `tsc`, which refuses an API above `lib`;\n`requires:` is what stops an agent from writing the code, hitting TS2550 and\nstarting over.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexis Regnaud <alexis.regnaud@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmrLHCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4CsQAJj0vKRdQfrAI51qPj+f3Ubn\nwOJmT0QQO2ngmh+NdryQVzfNKHHHEl3kJsZSeaMlnT5p0572uJTYO6vXCNqQfp3g\nW8UDT+iBA2L1TYDB2jf+/PWdWETTQao71lRFJrbTfP5Dwl7u92bFL/rWO+kvgLJc\n9RTxpr5mBnjhRjJ2kgNLy+WHq0BVu/0/Ou7tcIWwqWfyATreqSs05YhR9VDScfd5\npYpa3d1sJfndZMmcENOnnLEeAtZFJ4jq43p2tChWZq1q5CqvGyXE/tw85CAvvwmf\nBTAJz+blMnU9APFRKfLGbvePb3D6hs03whgMCuGgXR0onNOgGln2svimX862C96Q\ncIjsNeic+WJRG7saC79hGC4gtr72UizPLPsxQHALMkiz1iLNZdu2aSvQ+JlNZgLR\n6an6vmACPG